window.COURSE_TOPICS=[{"id": "1.1", "chapter": 1, "title": "Function and components", "html": "<p><em>Source: 14 pages, especially pp. 3-14.</em></p>\n<p>A physical circuit combines electrical devices for a purpose. Its circuit model replaces those devices with ideal elements and their connections. The battery-switch-lamp example illustrates the distinction: the physical battery, bulb, wires, and switch become an ideal source, load, connecting lines, and switch in the model. Closing the switch completes a conducting path; opening it interrupts the path.</p>\n<h3>Two functions of circuits</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Main purpose</th>\n<th>Example from the slides</th>\n<th>Main design emphasis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Power circuit</td>\n<td>Generate, transmit, and convert electrical energy</td>\n<td>Generator, step-up transformer, transmission line, step-down transformer, lights/heater/motor</td>\n<td>Efficient energy delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal circuit</td>\n<td>Transmit and process information carried by electrical signals</td>\n<td>Microphone, amplifier, processing stages, loudspeaker; telephone, CATV, network, Wi-Fi</td>\n<td>Signal quality and the required processing effect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both types have voltages, currents, and power. Classify them by their principal purpose. A battery lighting a bulb is a power circuit; the pictured temperature-measuring circuit is a signal circuit.</p>\n<h3>Three component groups</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Source:</strong> supplies electrical energy or an electrical signal. Its actual delivered power depends on the connected load.</li>\n<li><strong>Load:</strong> receives electrical energy and performs a useful conversion, such as producing light, heat, or motion.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate link:</strong> conductors, switches, control devices, protective components, and other transmission connections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the torch example, the battery is the source, the bulb is the load, and the switch and spring contacts belong to the intermediate link.</p>\n<p><strong>“Load increase”</strong> means increased power consumption. At an approximately fixed supply voltage, that requires increased load current. It does not generally mean that the resistance increases.</p>\n<h3>Terms to know</h3>\n<p>The course's introductory DC model has constant current and voltage; AC changes direction or polarity with time. Sinusoidal AC follows a sine wave. A two-terminal network connects to the rest of a circuit through two terminals. In the introductory classification used here, a passive network contains no source, whereas an active network contains a source.</p>\n<p><strong>Review:</strong> distinguish physical circuit/model, power/signal circuit, and passive/active network; identify the three component groups in an everyday circuit.</p>", "text": "  Source: 14 pages, especially pp. 3-14.  \n A physical circuit combines electrical devices for a purpose. Its circuit model replaces those devices with ideal elements and their connections. The battery-switch-lamp example illustrates the distinction: the physical battery, bulb, wires, and switch become an ideal source, load, connecting lines, and switch in the model. Closing the switch completes a conducting path; opening it interrupts the path. \n Two functions of circuits \n \n \n \n Type \n Main purpose \n Example from the slides \n Main design emphasis \n \n \n \n \n Power circuit \n Generate, transmit, and convert electrical energy \n Generator, step-up transformer, transmission line, step-down transformer, lights/heater/motor \n Efficient energy delivery \n \n \n Signal circuit \n Transmit and process information carried by electrical signals \n Microphone, amplifier, processing stages, loudspeaker; telephone, CATV, network, Wi-Fi \n Signal quality and the required processing effect \n \n \n \n Both types have voltages, currents, and power. Classify them by their principal purpose. A battery lighting a bulb is a power circuit; the pictured temperature-measuring circuit is a signal circuit. \n Three component groups \n \n  Source:  supplies electrical energy or an electrical signal. Its actual delivered power depends on the connected load. \n  Load:  receives electrical energy and performs a useful conversion, such as producing light, heat, or motion. \n  Intermediate link:  conductors, switches, control devices, protective components, and other transmission connections. \n \n For the torch example, the battery is the source, the bulb is the load, and the switch and spring contacts belong to the intermediate link. \n  “Load increase”  means increased power consumption. At an approximately fixed supply voltage, that requires increased load current. It does not generally mean that the resistance increases. \n Terms to know \n The course's introductory DC model has constant current and voltage; AC changes direction or polarity with time. Sinusoidal AC follows a sine wave. A two-terminal network connects to the rest of a circuit through two terminals. In the introductory classification used here, a passive network contains no source, whereas an active network contains a source. \n  Review:  distinguish physical circuit/model, power/signal circuit, and passive/active network; identify the three component groups in an everyday circuit. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.2", "chapter": 1, "title": "Basic quantities", "html": "<p><em>Source: 12 pages, especially pp. 3-12.</em></p>\n<p>The lesson compares six quantities: current, electric potential, voltage, electromotive force, power, and energy.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Course relationship and unit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Current <code>I</code></td>\n<td>Rate of charge flow past a point</td>\n<td>For constant current, <code>I = Q/t</code>; ampere, A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Potential <code>V_a</code></td>\n<td>Potential of node a relative to a chosen reference</td>\n<td><code>V_a = U_a0</code>; volt, V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voltage <code>U_ab</code></td>\n<td>Potential difference from a to b</td>\n<td><code>U_ab = V_a - V_b</code>; volt, V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EMF <code>E</code> or <code>U_s</code></td>\n<td>Energy supplied per unit charge by the source's non-electric energy-conversion process</td>\n<td>volt, V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power <code>P</code></td>\n<td>Rate of delivering or absorbing energy</td>\n<td>In the shown DC circuits, <code>P = UI</code>; watt, W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy <code>W</code></td>\n<td>Accumulated energy transfer</td>\n<td>Constant-power case: <code>W = Pt</code>; joule, J</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For varying current, the generalization of the first relationship is <code>i = dq/dt</code>. Conventional current follows positive-charge motion.</p>\n<h3>Potential depends on the reference; voltage differences do not</h3>\n<p><strong>Teacher example:</strong> a 10 V source supplies two 2 Ω series resistors. The current is <code>10/(2+2) = 2.5 A</code>, and each resistor drops 5 V.</p>\n<p>With the source's negative node c as ground, <code>V_a = 10 V</code>, <code>V_b = 5 V</code>, and <code>V_c = 0</code>. With node a as ground, <code>V_a = 0</code>, <code>V_b = -5 V</code>, and <code>V_c = -10 V</code>. In both choices, <code>U_ab = 5 V</code> and <code>U_bc = 5 V</code>.</p>\n<p>Without specifying a reference, those absolute node potentials cannot be uniquely stated. The voltage differences can still be determined.</p>\n<h3>EMF versus terminal voltage</h3>\n<p>Both are measured in volts. EMF describes the source's energy-conversion action; voltage is a potential difference between specified points. An ideal voltage source has terminal voltage equal to its prescribed source voltage. A practical source may have a lower terminal voltage while delivering current because of its internal resistance.</p>\n<h3>Power versus energy</h3>\n<p>Power measures how fast energy is transferred; energy also depends on duration. For source current leaving the positive terminal, <code>U_s I</code> is supplied power. For a load current entering its positive terminal, <code>U_L I</code> is absorbed power.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher questions, worked here:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bulb at 6 V and 1.6 A: <code>P = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 W</code>.</li>\n<li>Bulb at 2 V and 10 mA for 30 minutes: <code>P = 0.020 W</code>, <code>t = 1800 s</code>, so <code>W = 36 J</code>.</li>\n<li><code>1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not confuse kW, a power unit, with kWh, an energy unit.</p>", "text": "  Source: 12 pages, especially pp. 3-12.  \n The lesson compares six quantities: current, electric potential, voltage, electromotive force, power, and energy. \n \n \n \n Quantity \n Meaning \n Course relationship and unit \n \n \n \n \n Current  I  \n Rate of charge flow past a point \n For constant current,  I = Q/t ; ampere, A \n \n \n Potential  V_a  \n Potential of node a relative to a chosen reference \n  V_a = U_a0 ; volt, V \n \n \n Voltage  U_ab  \n Potential difference from a to b \n  U_ab = V_a - V_b ; volt, V \n \n \n EMF  E  or  U_s  \n Energy supplied per unit charge by the source's non-electric energy-conversion process \n volt, V \n \n \n Power  P  \n Rate of delivering or absorbing energy \n In the shown DC circuits,  P = UI ; watt, W \n \n \n Energy  W  \n Accumulated energy transfer \n Constant-power case:  W = Pt ; joule, J \n \n \n \n For varying current, the generalization of the first relationship is  i = dq/dt . Conventional current follows positive-charge motion. \n Potential depends on the reference; voltage differences do not \n  Teacher example:  a 10 V source supplies two 2 Ω series resistors. The current is  10/(2+2) = 2.5 A , and each resistor drops 5 V. \n With the source's negative node c as ground,  V_a = 10 V ,  V_b = 5 V , and  V_c = 0 . With node a as ground,  V_a = 0 ,  V_b = -5 V , and  V_c = -10 V . In both choices,  U_ab = 5 V  and  U_bc = 5 V . \n Without specifying a reference, those absolute node potentials cannot be uniquely stated. The voltage differences can still be determined. \n EMF versus terminal voltage \n Both are measured in volts. EMF describes the source's energy-conversion action; voltage is a potential difference between specified points. An ideal voltage source has terminal voltage equal to its prescribed source voltage. A practical source may have a lower terminal voltage while delivering current because of its internal resistance. \n Power versus energy \n Power measures how fast energy is transferred; energy also depends on duration. For source current leaving the positive terminal,  U_s I  is supplied power. For a load current entering its positive terminal,  U_L I  is absorbed power. \n  Teacher questions, worked here:  \n \n Bulb at 6 V and 1.6 A:  P = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 W . \n Bulb at 2 V and 10 mA for 30 minutes:  P = 0.020 W ,  t = 1800 s , so  W = 36 J . \n  1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J . \n \n Do not confuse kW, a power unit, with kWh, an energy unit. ", "minutes": 3}, {"id": "1.3", "chapter": 1, "title": "Circuit conditions", "html": "<p><em>Source: 7 pages, especially pp. 4-7.</em></p>\n<p>The slides analyze a source <code>U_s</code>, internal resistance <code>R₀</code>, and external load <code>R</code> in three operating conditions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Current</th>\n<th>Terminal voltage</th>\n<th>External power</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed/loaded</td>\n<td><code>I = U_s/(R₀ + R)</code></td>\n<td><code>U = IR = U_s - IR₀</code></td>\n<td><code>P = UI</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open/no load</td>\n<td><code>I = 0</code></td>\n<td>For this source model, <code>U_oc = U_s</code></td>\n<td><code>P = 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shorted terminals</td>\n<td><code>I_sc = U_s/R₀</code></td>\n<td><code>U = 0</code></td>\n<td><code>P_terminal = 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An open circuit has zero branch current, but its voltage can be nonzero. An ideal short has zero voltage, but its current can be nonzero. The open-circuit voltage and short-circuit current depend on the rest of the network.</p>\n<p><strong>Generated versus delivered power:</strong></p>\n<p><code>P_s = U_s I</code>, <code>ΔP = I²R₀</code>, and <code>P = UI = P_s - ΔP</code>.</p>\n<p>A short's zero external terminal power does not mean the source has stopped converting energy: internal heating can be substantial. The finite <code>I_sc</code> formula assumes nonzero internal resistance; an ideal zero-resistance voltage-source short has no finite-current solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example:</strong> <code>U_s = 125 V</code>, <code>U = 115 V</code>, <code>I = 4 A</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generated power: <code>P_s = 125 × 4 = 500 W</code>.</li>\n<li>Delivered power: <code>P = 115 × 4 = 460 W</code>.</li>\n<li>Internal voltage drop: <code>125 - 115 = 10 V</code>.</li>\n<li>Internal resistance: <code>R₀ = 10/4 = 2.5 Ω</code>.</li>\n<li>Internal loss: <code>ΔP = 40 W</code>, agreeing with <code>I²R₀</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Slide clarification:</strong> the example reuses a drawing with a shorting line, but its nonzero terminal voltage and numerical calculation describe loaded operation.</p>", "text": "  Source: 7 pages, especially pp. 4-7.  \n The slides analyze a source  U_s , internal resistance  R₀ , and external load  R  in three operating conditions. \n \n \n \n Condition \n Current \n Terminal voltage \n External power \n \n \n \n \n Closed/loaded \n  I = U_s/(R₀ + R)  \n  U = IR = U_s - IR₀  \n  P = UI  \n \n \n Open/no load \n  I = 0  \n For this source model,  U_oc = U_s  \n  P = 0  \n \n \n Shorted terminals \n  I_sc = U_s/R₀  \n  U = 0  \n  P_terminal = 0  \n \n \n \n An open circuit has zero branch current, but its voltage can be nonzero. An ideal short has zero voltage, but its current can be nonzero. The open-circuit voltage and short-circuit current depend on the rest of the network. \n  Generated versus delivered power:  \n  P_s = U_s I ,  ΔP = I²R₀ , and  P = UI = P_s - ΔP . \n A short's zero external terminal power does not mean the source has stopped converting energy: internal heating can be substantial. The finite  I_sc  formula assumes nonzero internal resistance; an ideal zero-resistance voltage-source short has no finite-current solution. \n  Teacher example:   U_s = 125 V ,  U = 115 V ,  I = 4 A . \n \n Generated power:  P_s = 125 × 4 = 500 W . \n Delivered power:  P = 115 × 4 = 460 W . \n Internal voltage drop:  125 - 115 = 10 V . \n Internal resistance:  R₀ = 10/4 = 2.5 Ω . \n Internal loss:  ΔP = 40 W , agreeing with  I²R₀ . \n \n  Slide clarification:  the example reuses a drawing with a shorting line, but its nonzero terminal voltage and numerical calculation describe loaded operation. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.4", "chapter": 1, "title": "Reference point and reference direction", "html": "<p><em>Source: 9 pages, all reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>Choose a reference node and set its potential to zero. This circuit “ground” is a reference choice; it need not be a connection to physical earth. Then <code>U_ab = V_a - V_b</code>, and <code>U_ba = -U_ab</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example 1:</strong> a 10 V source drives 4 Ω, 3 Ω, and 3 Ω in series. <code>I = 1 A</code>. With the bottom source terminal d grounded, <code>V_a = 10 V</code>, <code>V_b = 6 V</code>, and <code>V_c = 3 V</code>. Without a ground choice, only differences are determined.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example 2:</strong> two equal series resistors connect between nodes at <code>V_a = 12 V</code> and <code>V_b = -10 V</code>. Their midpoint has</p>\n<p><code>V_c = V_b + (V_a - V_b)/2 = -10 + 11 = 1 V</code>.</p>\n<h3>Reference directions</h3>\n<p>Assign a current arrow and a voltage reference before calculating. A positive answer agrees with the reference; a negative answer indicates the opposite actual direction or polarity.</p>\n<p>The teacher represents voltage direction in three equivalent ways: an arrow, <code>+/-</code> terminal marks, or ordered subscripts such as <code>U_AB</code>. In the course's arrow convention, the voltage arrow points from the referenced positive terminal toward the referenced negative terminal.</p>\n<p>For a resistor, <code>U = RI</code> when current enters the terminal marked positive. If its current reference is reversed while the voltage reference is retained, use <code>U = -RI</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam habit:</strong> never change an arrow halfway through a calculation without also changing the associated signed variable.</p>", "text": "  Source: 9 pages, all reviewed.  \n Choose a reference node and set its potential to zero. This circuit “ground” is a reference choice; it need not be a connection to physical earth. Then  U_ab = V_a - V_b , and  U_ba = -U_ab . \n  Teacher example 1:  a 10 V source drives 4 Ω, 3 Ω, and 3 Ω in series.  I = 1 A . With the bottom source terminal d grounded,  V_a = 10 V ,  V_b = 6 V , and  V_c = 3 V . Without a ground choice, only differences are determined. \n  Teacher example 2:  two equal series resistors connect between nodes at  V_a = 12 V  and  V_b = -10 V . Their midpoint has \n  V_c = V_b + (V_a - V_b)/2 = -10 + 11 = 1 V . \n Reference directions \n Assign a current arrow and a voltage reference before calculating. A positive answer agrees with the reference; a negative answer indicates the opposite actual direction or polarity. \n The teacher represents voltage direction in three equivalent ways: an arrow,  +/-  terminal marks, or ordered subscripts such as  U_AB . In the course's arrow convention, the voltage arrow points from the referenced positive terminal toward the referenced negative terminal. \n For a resistor,  U = RI  when current enters the terminal marked positive. If its current reference is reversed while the voltage reference is retained, use  U = -RI . \n  Exam habit:  never change an arrow halfway through a calculation without also changing the associated signed variable. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.5", "chapter": 1, "title": "Ideal circuit elements", "html": "<p><em>Source: 16 pages, especially pp. 2-16.</em></p>\n<p>The course separates passive elements R, L, C from ideal active voltage and current sources.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Law with passive voltage/current references</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Resistor</td>\n<td><code>u = Ri</code></td>\n<td>Voltage is proportional to current</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inductor</td>\n<td><code>u_L = L di_L/dt</code></td>\n<td>Voltage depends on current's rate of change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitor</td>\n<td><code>i_C = dq/dt = C du_C/dt</code></td>\n<td>Current depends on voltage's rate of change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The inductor diagram also shows its induced EMF <code>e</code>, with <code>u_L = -e</code>; the opposing EMF is consistent with Lenz's law.</p>\n<h3>Ideal sources</h3>\n<p>An independent ideal voltage source fixes its prescribed voltage; the external circuit determines its current. An independent ideal current source fixes its prescribed current; the external circuit determines its voltage. The examples use constant sources, though prescribed sources can also vary with time.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher voltage-source example:</strong> a 10 V source supplies one 2 Ω resistor at 5 A. Adding a second 2 Ω resistor in parallel makes the total current 10 A; the source voltage remains 10 V.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher current-source example:</strong> a 1 A source gives 1 V across 1 Ω and 10 V across 10 Ω. The source current remains 1 A.</p>\n<h3>A source can absorb power</h3>\n<p>Use the passive convention for <strong>absorbed</strong> power: <code>p_abs = ui</code> when the referenced current enters the positive voltage terminal. Negative absorbed power means delivery. Identify actual direction or use signed variables consistently; the word “source” alone does not determine the power sign.</p>\n<p>The teacher's five power questions are completed below. Each uses an upward 3 A current source and a voltage source with 3 V, positive at the top.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Circuit in the slide</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Resistor absorption</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Voltage-source absorption</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Current-source delivery</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Only the two sources, connected in one loop</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add a 1 Ω resistor in parallel</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add a 0.5 Ω resistor in parallel</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">18 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">-9 W, so it supplies 9 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add a 2 Ω resistor in parallel</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4.5 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4.5 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put a 1 Ω resistor in series along the top connection</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">9 W</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">18 W</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Worked here:</strong> the parallel cases follow <code>I_R = 3/R</code>; the voltage-source downward current is <code>3 - I_R</code>. In the series case the resistor drops 3 V, so the current source sustains 6 V and supplies 18 W. Every row satisfies power conservation.</p>", "text": "  Source: 16 pages, especially pp. 2-16.  \n The course separates passive elements R, L, C from ideal active voltage and current sources. \n \n \n \n Element \n Law with passive voltage/current references \n Meaning \n \n \n \n \n Resistor \n  u = Ri  \n Voltage is proportional to current \n \n \n Inductor \n  u_L = L di_L/dt  \n Voltage depends on current's rate of change \n \n \n Capacitor \n  i_C = dq/dt = C du_C/dt  \n Current depends on voltage's rate of change \n \n \n \n The inductor diagram also shows its induced EMF  e , with  u_L = -e ; the opposing EMF is consistent with Lenz's law. \n Ideal sources \n An independent ideal voltage source fixes its prescribed voltage; the external circuit determines its current. An independent ideal current source fixes its prescribed current; the external circuit determines its voltage. The examples use constant sources, though prescribed sources can also vary with time. \n  Teacher voltage-source example:  a 10 V source supplies one 2 Ω resistor at 5 A. Adding a second 2 Ω resistor in parallel makes the total current 10 A; the source voltage remains 10 V. \n  Teacher current-source example:  a 1 A source gives 1 V across 1 Ω and 10 V across 10 Ω. The source current remains 1 A. \n A source can absorb power \n Use the passive convention for  absorbed  power:  p_abs = ui  when the referenced current enters the positive voltage terminal. Negative absorbed power means delivery. Identify actual direction or use signed variables consistently; the word “source” alone does not determine the power sign. \n The teacher's five power questions are completed below. Each uses an upward 3 A current source and a voltage source with 3 V, positive at the top. \n \n \n \n Circuit in the slide \n Resistor absorption \n Voltage-source absorption \n Current-source delivery \n \n \n \n \n Only the two sources, connected in one loop \n - \n 9 W \n 9 W \n \n \n Add a 1 Ω resistor in parallel \n 9 W \n 0 W \n 9 W \n \n \n Add a 0.5 Ω resistor in parallel \n 18 W \n -9 W, so it supplies 9 W \n 9 W \n \n \n Add a 2 Ω resistor in parallel \n 4.5 W \n 4.5 W \n 9 W \n \n \n Put a 1 Ω resistor in series along the top connection \n 9 W \n 9 W \n 18 W \n \n \n \n  Worked here:  the parallel cases follow  I_R = 3/R ; the voltage-source downward current is  3 - I_R . In the series case the resistor drops 3 V, so the current source sustains 6 V and supplies 18 W. Every row satisfies power conservation. ", "minutes": 3}, {"id": "1.6", "chapter": 1, "title": "Practical sources and source transformation", "html": "<p><em>Source: 18 pages, including the worked example and final review.</em></p>\n<p>A practical voltage source has an ideal voltage source in series with resistance:</p>\n<p><code>U = U_s - IR</code>.</p>\n<p>Its terminal voltage falls as delivered current increases. A practical current source has an ideal current source in parallel with resistance:</p>\n<p><code>I_out = I_s - U/R</code>.</p>\n<p>The two descriptions have the same external terminal behavior when</p>\n<p><code>I_s = U_s/R</code>, <code>U_s = I_s R</code>, and the resistance is unchanged.</p>\n<p>The current-source arrow points toward the voltage source's positive terminal. For finite nonzero R, change series resistance into parallel resistance, or the reverse.</p>\n<p><strong>Conditions emphasized in the slides:</strong> transform practical source combinations, not isolated ideal sources into finite equivalents. Equivalence is external: internal currents, powers, and component behavior can differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example:</strong> a 10 V source in series with 2 Ω becomes a 5 A source in parallel with 2 Ω. Connecting a 3 Ω load gives <code>I_L = 10/(2+3) = 2 A</code> in either representation. In the current-source version, current division gives the same result.</p>\n<p>For two parallel resistors carrying total current I:</p>\n<p><code>I₁ = [R₂/(R₁+R₂)]I</code>, <code>I₂ = [R₁/(R₁+R₂)]I</code>.</p>\n<h3>Main course example</h3>\n<p>The slides show a 5 V source, a 2 kΩ resistor feeding a central node, a 1 kΩ shunt, a 2 mA source injecting into the node, and a series path of 1 kΩ plus the 2 kΩ load. There is also a 2 kΩ resistor directly across the ideal voltage source and a 2 kΩ resistor in series with the ideal current source.</p>\n<p>For the external load calculation, the last two resistors can be omitted: a parallel resistor cannot change an ideal voltage source's imposed terminal voltage, and a series resistor cannot change an ideal current source's imposed current. This simplification does not preserve their internal power.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Transform <code>5 V + series 2 kΩ</code> into <code>2.5 mA || 2 kΩ</code>.</li>\n<li>Combine the same-direction current sources: <code>2.5 + 2 = 4.5 mA</code>.</li>\n<li>Combine the shunts: <code>2 kΩ || 1 kΩ = 2/3 kΩ</code>.</li>\n<li>The external branch is <code>1 + 2 = 3 kΩ</code>.</li>\n<li>Current division gives <code>I_load = 4.5 × (2/3)/(3+2/3) = 9/11 mA ≈ 0.818 mA</code>, downward.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Final review circuit, worked here:</strong> a <code>40 V + 2 Ω</code> branch and a <code>20 V + 2 Ω</code> branch supply a 4 Ω load in parallel. Transform them into 20 A and 10 A sources; combine to <code>30 A || 1 Ω</code>. The load current is <code>30 × 1/(1+4) = 6 A</code>, downward, with 24 V across the load.</p>", "text": "  Source: 18 pages, including the worked example and final review.  \n A practical voltage source has an ideal voltage source in series with resistance: \n  U = U_s - IR . \n Its terminal voltage falls as delivered current increases. A practical current source has an ideal current source in parallel with resistance: \n  I_out = I_s - U/R . \n The two descriptions have the same external terminal behavior when \n  I_s = U_s/R ,  U_s = I_s R , and the resistance is unchanged. \n The current-source arrow points toward the voltage source's positive terminal. For finite nonzero R, change series resistance into parallel resistance, or the reverse. \n  Conditions emphasized in the slides:  transform practical source combinations, not isolated ideal sources into finite equivalents. Equivalence is external: internal currents, powers, and component behavior can differ. \n  Teacher example:  a 10 V source in series with 2 Ω becomes a 5 A source in parallel with 2 Ω. Connecting a 3 Ω load gives  I_L = 10/(2+3) = 2 A  in either representation. In the current-source version, current division gives the same result. \n For two parallel resistors carrying total current I: \n  I₁ = [R₂/(R₁+R₂)]I ,  I₂ = [R₁/(R₁+R₂)]I . \n Main course example \n The slides show a 5 V source, a 2 kΩ resistor feeding a central node, a 1 kΩ shunt, a 2 mA source injecting into the node, and a series path of 1 kΩ plus the 2 kΩ load. There is also a 2 kΩ resistor directly across the ideal voltage source and a 2 kΩ resistor in series with the ideal current source. \n For the external load calculation, the last two resistors can be omitted: a parallel resistor cannot change an ideal voltage source's imposed terminal voltage, and a series resistor cannot change an ideal current source's imposed current. This simplification does not preserve their internal power. \n \n Transform  5 V + series 2 kΩ  into  2.5 mA || 2 kΩ . \n Combine the same-direction current sources:  2.5 + 2 = 4.5 mA . \n Combine the shunts:  2 kΩ || 1 kΩ = 2/3 kΩ . \n The external branch is  1 + 2 = 3 kΩ . \n Current division gives  I_load = 4.5 × (2/3)/(3+2/3) = 9/11 mA ≈ 0.818 mA , downward. \n \n  Final review circuit, worked here:  a  40 V + 2 Ω  branch and a  20 V + 2 Ω  branch supply a 4 Ω load in parallel. Transform them into 20 A and 10 A sources; combine to  30 A || 1 Ω . The load current is  30 × 1/(1+4) = 6 A , downward, with 24 V across the load. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.7", "chapter": 1, "title": "Kirchhoff's Laws", "html": "<p><em>Source: 31 pages, including topology exercises, generalized nodes, and generalized loops.</em></p>\n<p>KCL and KVL apply to the usual lumped DC and AC models, including nonlinear elements. Linearity is a requirement of superposition, not a requirement for these conservation laws.</p>\n<h3>Topology vocabulary</h3>\n<p>The teacher uses <strong>node</strong> for an essential junction where three or more elements meet and <strong>branch</strong> for the current path between such nodes; a branch can contain several series elements. A loop is any closed path. The slide calls a mesh an independent loop; in standard planar-circuit terminology, a mesh is a loop with no smaller loop inside it. A suitable set of meshes provides independent loop equations.</p>\n<p>The four counting examples give:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Slide example</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Essential nodes</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Branches</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Closed loops</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Meshes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Three parallel paths</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Four parallel paths</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bridge network</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Five-branch network used again in 1.8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Count connected conductors as one node. Distinguish all possible loops from an independent equation set.</p>\n<h3>KCL: current balance</h3>\n<p>The algebraic sum of currents at a node is zero: <code>Σi = 0</code>. With entering currents positive, the teacher's five-current example gives</p>\n<p><code>I₁ + I₃ - I₂ - I₄ - I₅ = 0</code>, or <code>I₁ + I₃ = I₂ + I₄ + I₅</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Generalized node:</strong> draw a closed boundary around several components. Internal currents cancel; the currents crossing its boundary still balance. The transistor and triangular-network examples give <code>I₁ + I₂ = I₃</code>. In the final two-junction example, <code>I₁ + I₂ = I₃ = I₄ + I₅</code>.</p>\n<h3>KVL: voltage balance</h3>\n<p>The algebraic sum of voltage changes around a closed path is zero: <code>Σu = 0</code>. Choose a traversal direction and apply the polarity signs consistently. For the four-voltage rectangle in the slides:</p>\n<p><code>U₁ - U₂ + U₃ - U₄ = 0</code>.</p>\n<p>For the opposing-source/resistor example, the chosen traversal gives</p>\n<p><code>U_s2 + R₂I₂ + R₁I₁ - U_s1 = 0</code>.</p>\n<p>Reversing the traversal reverses every sign and gives an equivalent equation.</p>\n<p><strong>Generalized loop:</strong> an imaginary closing path can include the terminal voltage between two points. A branch consisting of a positive-at-top source and a series resistor has <code>U = U_s + RI</code> if I enters its top terminal, and <code>U = U_s - RI</code> if I leaves its top terminal. The difference comes from the current reference, not from changing the source itself.</p>", "text": "  Source: 31 pages, including topology exercises, generalized nodes, and generalized loops.  \n KCL and KVL apply to the usual lumped DC and AC models, including nonlinear elements. Linearity is a requirement of superposition, not a requirement for these conservation laws. \n Topology vocabulary \n The teacher uses  node  for an essential junction where three or more elements meet and  branch  for the current path between such nodes; a branch can contain several series elements. A loop is any closed path. The slide calls a mesh an independent loop; in standard planar-circuit terminology, a mesh is a loop with no smaller loop inside it. A suitable set of meshes provides independent loop equations. \n The four counting examples give: \n \n \n \n Slide example \n Essential nodes \n Branches \n Closed loops \n Meshes \n \n \n \n \n Three parallel paths \n 2 \n 3 \n 3 \n 2 \n \n \n Four parallel paths \n 2 \n 4 \n 6 \n 3 \n \n \n Bridge network \n 4 \n 6 \n 7 \n 3 \n \n \n Five-branch network used again in 1.8 \n 3 \n 5 \n 6 \n 3 \n \n \n \n Count connected conductors as one node. Distinguish all possible loops from an independent equation set. \n KCL: current balance \n The algebraic sum of currents at a node is zero:  Σi = 0 . With entering currents positive, the teacher's five-current example gives \n  I₁ + I₃ - I₂ - I₄ - I₅ = 0 , or  I₁ + I₃ = I₂ + I₄ + I₅ . \n  Generalized node:  draw a closed boundary around several components. Internal currents cancel; the currents crossing its boundary still balance. The transistor and triangular-network examples give  I₁ + I₂ = I₃ . In the final two-junction example,  I₁ + I₂ = I₃ = I₄ + I₅ . \n KVL: voltage balance \n The algebraic sum of voltage changes around a closed path is zero:  Σu = 0 . Choose a traversal direction and apply the polarity signs consistently. For the four-voltage rectangle in the slides: \n  U₁ - U₂ + U₃ - U₄ = 0 . \n For the opposing-source/resistor example, the chosen traversal gives \n  U_s2 + R₂I₂ + R₁I₁ - U_s1 = 0 . \n Reversing the traversal reverses every sign and gives an equivalent equation. \n  Generalized loop:  an imaginary closing path can include the terminal voltage between two points. A branch consisting of a positive-at-top source and a series resistor has  U = U_s + RI  if I enters its top terminal, and  U = U_s - RI  if I leaves its top terminal. The difference comes from the current reference, not from changing the source itself. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.8", "chapter": 1, "title": "Branch Current Analysis", "html": "<p><em>Source: 11 pages, all reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>The purpose is to find the current in each branch of a complex network using KCL, KVL, and element laws.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assign branch-current references and loop traversal directions.</li>\n<li>Write KCL at independent nodes.</li>\n<li>Write KVL around independent loops or meshes.</li>\n<li>Solve the simultaneous equations and interpret the signs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For a connected graph with n nodes and b branches, there are <code>n-1</code> independent KCL equations and <code>b-n+1</code> independent loop equations. Apply these counts consistently with the graph/branch definition.</p>\n<h3>Teacher's five-branch example</h3>\n<p><img alt=\"Original course slide, lesson 1.8, page 8: assigned branch-current references.\" src=\"assets/branch-current-references.png\" /></p>\n<p><em>Original course slide, lesson 1.8, page 8: assigned branch-current references.</em></p>\n<p>The bottom node is common. At left top node a, <code>I₁</code> arrives upward through <code>U_s1, R₁</code>, <code>I₃</code> leaves downward through <code>R₃</code>, and <code>I₅</code> crosses <code>R₅</code> to right top node b. At b, <code>I₂</code> arrives upward through <code>U_s2, R₂</code>, and <code>I₄</code> leaves downward through <code>R₄</code>.</p>\n<p>Use these two independent node equations:</p>\n<p><code>I₁ - I₃ - I₅ = 0</code></p>\n<p><code>I₅ + I₂ - I₄ = 0</code>.</p>\n<p>The bottom-node equation <code>I₃ + I₄ - I₁ - I₂ = 0</code> follows from them and adds no independent information.</p>\n<p>The three loop equations are:</p>\n<p><code>I₃R₃ + I₁R₁ - U_s1 = 0</code></p>\n<p><code>I₅R₅ + I₄R₄ - I₃R₃ = 0</code></p>\n<p><code>U_s2 - I₂R₂ - I₄R₄ = 0</code>.</p>\n<p>There are five independent equations for five branch currents. The slides give symbolic component values, so the intended result is the equation system, not a particular numerical current.</p>", "text": "  Source: 11 pages, all reviewed.  \n The purpose is to find the current in each branch of a complex network using KCL, KVL, and element laws. \n \n Assign branch-current references and loop traversal directions. \n Write KCL at independent nodes. \n Write KVL around independent loops or meshes. \n Solve the simultaneous equations and interpret the signs. \n \n For a connected graph with n nodes and b branches, there are  n-1  independent KCL equations and  b-n+1  independent loop equations. Apply these counts consistently with the graph/branch definition. \n Teacher's five-branch example \n   \n  Original course slide, lesson 1.8, page 8: assigned branch-current references.  \n The bottom node is common. At left top node a,  I₁  arrives upward through  U_s1, R₁ ,  I₃  leaves downward through  R₃ , and  I₅  crosses  R₅  to right top node b. At b,  I₂  arrives upward through  U_s2, R₂ , and  I₄  leaves downward through  R₄ . \n Use these two independent node equations: \n  I₁ - I₃ - I₅ = 0  \n  I₅ + I₂ - I₄ = 0 . \n The bottom-node equation  I₃ + I₄ - I₁ - I₂ = 0  follows from them and adds no independent information. \n The three loop equations are: \n  I₃R₃ + I₁R₁ - U_s1 = 0  \n  I₅R₅ + I₄R₄ - I₃R₃ = 0  \n  U_s2 - I₂R₂ - I₄R₄ = 0 . \n There are five independent equations for five branch currents. The slides give symbolic component values, so the intended result is the equation system, not a particular numerical current. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.9", "chapter": 1, "title": "Superposition theorem", "html": "<p><em>Source: 15 pages, including five examples.</em></p>\n<p>In a linear network, a branch voltage or current is the algebraic sum of the responses produced by the independent sources acting separately. The purpose is to simplify calculation.</p>\n<p>To analyze one source's contribution, replace every other independent ideal voltage source with a short and every other independent ideal current source with an open. Preserve internal resistances. If dependent sources occur in another problem, retain them; the course examples use independent sources.</p>\n<p>Repeat for the remaining sources and add signed currents or voltages using the same references. Sources may also be divided into groups; each contribution need not contain exactly one source.</p>\n<p><strong>Power cannot be superposed:</strong> <code>R(I₁+I₂)²</code> is not generally <code>RI₁² + RI₂²</code>.</p>\n<h3>Course examples</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Essential setup</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>E1</td>\n<td>Two voltage-source branches and a central resistor</td>\n<td>Add each branch's two signed contributions separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E2</td>\n<td>Upward 4 A source with a series 10 Ω branch; central 10 Ω; right 10 Ω and a 20 V source positive at the bottom</td>\n<td>Central downward current <code>2 A + (-1 A) = 1 A</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E3</td>\n<td>7 A source, 35 V source, and 1 Ω, 2 Ω, 3 Ω, 4 Ω network</td>\n<td>Marked rightward 4 Ω current <code>3 A + 5 A = 8 A</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E4</td>\n<td>3 A upward left source, 5 A downward right source, 10 V bottom source, 2 Ω and 3 Ω middle paths, 4 Ω upper path</td>\n<td><strong>Worked here:</strong> marked bottom leftward current <code>6.2 A</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E5</td>\n<td>18 V + 1 Ω and 9 V + 1 Ω branches in parallel with a 4 Ω load</td>\n<td><strong>Worked here:</strong> downward load current <code>2 A + 1 A = 3 A</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>E4 check:</strong> set the right bottom node to 0 V and the left bottom node to 10 V. KCL at the top requires 2 A from the middle node upward through 4 Ω. The middle-node equation is <code>(V_m-10)/2 + V_m/3 + 2 = 0</code>, giving <code>V_m = 3.6 V</code>. The left node supplies <code>3 A + (10-3.6)/2 = 6.2 A</code>, matching the indicated bottom return-current direction.</p>\n<p><strong>E5 check:</strong> with only 18 V active, the other 1 Ω branch remains after its source is shorted. The top node is 8 V and the load gets 2 A. With only 9 V active, the node is 4 V and the load gets 1 A. Together it is 12 V and 3 A.</p>", "text": "  Source: 15 pages, including five examples.  \n In a linear network, a branch voltage or current is the algebraic sum of the responses produced by the independent sources acting separately. The purpose is to simplify calculation. \n To analyze one source's contribution, replace every other independent ideal voltage source with a short and every other independent ideal current source with an open. Preserve internal resistances. If dependent sources occur in another problem, retain them; the course examples use independent sources. \n Repeat for the remaining sources and add signed currents or voltages using the same references. Sources may also be divided into groups; each contribution need not contain exactly one source. \n  Power cannot be superposed:   R(I₁+I₂)²  is not generally  RI₁² + RI₂² . \n Course examples \n \n \n \n Example \n Essential setup \n Result \n \n \n \n \n E1 \n Two voltage-source branches and a central resistor \n Add each branch's two signed contributions separately \n \n \n E2 \n Upward 4 A source with a series 10 Ω branch; central 10 Ω; right 10 Ω and a 20 V source positive at the bottom \n Central downward current  2 A + (-1 A) = 1 A  \n \n \n E3 \n 7 A source, 35 V source, and 1 Ω, 2 Ω, 3 Ω, 4 Ω network \n Marked rightward 4 Ω current  3 A + 5 A = 8 A  \n \n \n E4 \n 3 A upward left source, 5 A downward right source, 10 V bottom source, 2 Ω and 3 Ω middle paths, 4 Ω upper path \n  Worked here:  marked bottom leftward current  6.2 A  \n \n \n E5 \n 18 V + 1 Ω and 9 V + 1 Ω branches in parallel with a 4 Ω load \n  Worked here:  downward load current  2 A + 1 A = 3 A  \n \n \n \n  E4 check:  set the right bottom node to 0 V and the left bottom node to 10 V. KCL at the top requires 2 A from the middle node upward through 4 Ω. The middle-node equation is  (V_m-10)/2 + V_m/3 + 2 = 0 , giving  V_m = 3.6 V . The left node supplies  3 A + (10-3.6)/2 = 6.2 A , matching the indicated bottom return-current direction. \n  E5 check:  with only 18 V active, the other 1 Ω branch remains after its source is shorted. The top node is 8 V and the load gets 2 A. With only 9 V active, the node is 4 V and the load gets 1 A. Together it is 12 V and 3 A. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "1.10", "chapter": 1, "title": "Equivalent Source Theorem", "html": "<p><em>Source: 26 pages, including Thévenin and Norton treatments of the same circuit.</em></p>\n<p>Replace an active linear two-terminal network by a simpler network that has the same terminal voltage-current relationship.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Equivalent</th>\n<th>Source value</th>\n<th>Resistance connection</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Thévenin</td>\n<td><code>U_th = U_oc</code>, the open-circuit voltage at the selected port</td>\n<td><code>R₀</code> in series</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Norton</td>\n<td><code>I_N = I_sc</code>, the short-circuit current at that port</td>\n<td>The same <code>R₀</code> in parallel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Find <code>R₀</code> by suppressing independent sources and looking into the port. When the values are finite and the ratio is defined, <code>R₀ = U_oc/I_sc</code>. In networks containing dependent sources, a test source may be needed; do not suppress dependent sources.</p>\n<p>Remove <strong>only the external load</strong> before calculating the equivalent. The selected port can lie between two nodes neither of which is the ground node.</p>\n<h3>Teacher's 8 Ω bridge-load example</h3>\n<p><img alt=\"Original course slide, lesson 1.10, page 6: the selected port after removing the 8 Ω load.\" src=\"assets/thevenin-load-port.png\" /></p>\n<p><em>Original course slide, lesson 1.10, page 6: the selected port after removing the 8 Ω load.</em></p>\n<p>The 8 Ω load connects nodes a and b. On the left, an 8 V source with series 2 Ω is in parallel with another 2 Ω. On the right, a downward 3 A source is in parallel with a 5 V source and series 5 Ω. The bottom connection stays in place when the 8 Ω load is removed.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Open-circuit voltage:</strong> the left divider gives <code>V_a = 4 V</code>. The right node has <code>V_b = -10 V</code>. Therefore <code>U_ab = 4 - (-10) = 14 V</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Equivalent resistance:</strong> short both ideal voltage sources and open the ideal current source. Looking from a to b gives <code>R₀ = (2 Ω || 2 Ω) + 5 Ω = 6 Ω</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Thévenin load current:</strong> <code>I = 14/(6+8) = 1 A</code>, from a to b.</li>\n<li><strong>Norton equivalent:</strong> <code>I_N = 14/6 A</code>, in parallel with 6 Ω. Current division through the 8 Ω load again gives 1 A.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The two methods describe the same external behavior. Choose whichever source representation makes the load calculation simpler.</p>", "text": "  Source: 26 pages, including Thévenin and Norton treatments of the same circuit.  \n Replace an active linear two-terminal network by a simpler network that has the same terminal voltage-current relationship. \n \n \n \n Equivalent \n Source value \n Resistance connection \n \n \n \n \n Thévenin \n  U_th = U_oc , the open-circuit voltage at the selected port \n  R₀  in series \n \n \n Norton \n  I_N = I_sc , the short-circuit current at that port \n The same  R₀  in parallel \n \n \n \n Find  R₀  by suppressing independent sources and looking into the port. When the values are finite and the ratio is defined,  R₀ = U_oc/I_sc . In networks containing dependent sources, a test source may be needed; do not suppress dependent sources. \n Remove  only the external load  before calculating the equivalent. The selected port can lie between two nodes neither of which is the ground node. \n Teacher's 8 Ω bridge-load example \n   \n  Original course slide, lesson 1.10, page 6: the selected port after removing the 8 Ω load.  \n The 8 Ω load connects nodes a and b. On the left, an 8 V source with series 2 Ω is in parallel with another 2 Ω. On the right, a downward 3 A source is in parallel with a 5 V source and series 5 Ω. The bottom connection stays in place when the 8 Ω load is removed. \n \n  Open-circuit voltage:  the left divider gives  V_a = 4 V . The right node has  V_b = -10 V . Therefore  U_ab = 4 - (-10) = 14 V . \n  Equivalent resistance:  short both ideal voltage sources and open the ideal current source. Looking from a to b gives  R₀ = (2 Ω || 2 Ω) + 5 Ω = 6 Ω . \n  Thévenin load current:   I = 14/(6+8) = 1 A , from a to b. \n  Norton equivalent:   I_N = 14/6 A , in parallel with 6 Ω. Current division through the 8 Ω load again gives 1 A. \n \n The two methods describe the same external behavior. Choose whichever source representation makes the load calculation simpler. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "2.1", "chapter": 2, "title": "Basic concept", "html": "<p><em>Source: 25 pages, all reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>A transient is the interval during which a circuit changes from one operating state to another. For the DC circuits in this lesson, a steady state has constant currents and voltages after the effects of switching have settled. In AC, steady state may instead be periodic; “steady” does not universally mean constant.</p>\n<p>Switching can change the circuit's connections, component parameters, or power supply. A nontrivial transient occurs when the change requires stored energy to redistribute over time.</p>\n<h3>The three-lamp demonstration</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch</th>\n<th>Observation after the switch closes</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Resistor and lamp</td>\n<td>Lamp lights immediately and remains steady</td>\n<td>The ideal resistor stores no field energy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inductor and lamp</td>\n<td>Lamp gradually becomes bright</td>\n<td>Inductor current builds continuously</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitor and lamp</td>\n<td>Lamp initially flashes, then fades</td>\n<td>Charging current decreases as capacitor voltage rises</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For an initially unenergized simple RC circuit, capacitor voltage rises from 0 toward <code>U_s</code>. For a simple RL circuit, inductor current rises from 0 toward <code>U_s/R</code>. A purely resistive ideal circuit can move immediately to its new algebraic operating point.</p>\n<h3>Why the capacitor voltage and inductor current matter</h3>\n<p>A capacitor stores electric-field energy:</p>\n<p><code>W_e = ½ C u_C²</code>.</p>\n<p>An inductor stores magnetic-field energy:</p>\n<p><code>W_m = ½ L i_L²</code>.</p>\n<p>For ordinary finite excitation, these state variables cannot jump instantaneously. A capacitor voltage jump would require an impulsive current; an inductor current jump would require an impulsive voltage. That is why the chapter focuses on <code>u_C</code> and <code>i_L</code>.</p>\n<h3>Applications included in the actual slides</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RC time delay:</strong> the capacitor takes time to reach a chosen voltage threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Inductive spark generator:</strong> closing the circuit builds magnetic energy; interrupting its current produces a high induced voltage that can form a spark.</li>\n<li><strong>Fluorescent-lamp starting:</strong> the starter and inductive ballast use a transient to help initiate discharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Equipment behavior:</strong> transients can create large voltages or currents. Studying them allows useful effects to be designed and unwanted stresses to be controlled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The idealized “energy cannot change suddenly” explanation presumes an ordinary switching event with finite excitation, not a deliberately imposed ideal impulse.</p>", "text": "  Source: 25 pages, all reviewed.  \n A transient is the interval during which a circuit changes from one operating state to another. For the DC circuits in this lesson, a steady state has constant currents and voltages after the effects of switching have settled. In AC, steady state may instead be periodic; “steady” does not universally mean constant. \n Switching can change the circuit's connections, component parameters, or power supply. A nontrivial transient occurs when the change requires stored energy to redistribute over time. \n The three-lamp demonstration \n \n \n \n Branch \n Observation after the switch closes \n Explanation \n \n \n \n \n Resistor and lamp \n Lamp lights immediately and remains steady \n The ideal resistor stores no field energy \n \n \n Inductor and lamp \n Lamp gradually becomes bright \n Inductor current builds continuously \n \n \n Capacitor and lamp \n Lamp initially flashes, then fades \n Charging current decreases as capacitor voltage rises \n \n \n \n For an initially unenergized simple RC circuit, capacitor voltage rises from 0 toward  U_s . For a simple RL circuit, inductor current rises from 0 toward  U_s/R . A purely resistive ideal circuit can move immediately to its new algebraic operating point. \n Why the capacitor voltage and inductor current matter \n A capacitor stores electric-field energy: \n  W_e = ½ C u_C² . \n An inductor stores magnetic-field energy: \n  W_m = ½ L i_L² . \n For ordinary finite excitation, these state variables cannot jump instantaneously. A capacitor voltage jump would require an impulsive current; an inductor current jump would require an impulsive voltage. That is why the chapter focuses on  u_C  and  i_L . \n Applications included in the actual slides \n \n  RC time delay:  the capacitor takes time to reach a chosen voltage threshold. \n  Inductive spark generator:  closing the circuit builds magnetic energy; interrupting its current produces a high induced voltage that can form a spark. \n  Fluorescent-lamp starting:  the starter and inductive ballast use a transient to help initiate discharge. \n  Equipment behavior:  transients can create large voltages or currents. Studying them allows useful effects to be designed and unwanted stresses to be controlled. \n \n The idealized “energy cannot change suddenly” explanation presumes an ordinary switching event with finite excitation, not a deliberately imposed ideal impulse. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "2.2", "chapter": 2, "title": "Switching law", "html": "<p><em>Source: 9 pages, including three example circuits.</em></p>\n<p>Set the switching event to <code>t = 0</code>. Use <code>0⁻</code> for immediately before it and <code>0⁺</code> for immediately after it.</p>\n<p><code>u_C(0⁺) = u_C(0⁻)</code></p>\n<p><code>i_L(0⁺) = i_L(0⁻)</code>.</p>\n<p>Continuity at one instant does not mean the quantity never changes afterward. Capacitor current and inductor voltage are not subject to these continuity constraints and can jump.</p>\n<h3>DC steady-state replacements</h3>\n<p>Once a DC steady state is established:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capacitor: open circuit, because <code>du_C/dt = 0</code> gives <code>i_C = 0</code>.</li>\n<li>Inductor: short circuit, because <code>di_L/dt = 0</code> gives <code>u_L = 0</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are <strong>steady-state</strong> replacements. At <code>0⁺</code>, retain the storage state instead of automatically replacing an inductor with a short or a capacitor with an open.</p>\n<h3>Course examples</h3>\n<p><strong>E1: combined steady-state circuit.</strong> A 6 V source feeds a 2 Ω resistor, followed by three parallel branches: 2 Ω, 2 Ω plus L, and C. In DC steady state, L shorts and C opens. The two remaining branches give <code>2 Ω || 2 Ω = 1 Ω</code>, so total current is <code>6/(2+1) = 2 A</code>. Each conducting branch carries 1 A, capacitor current is zero, capacitor voltage is 2 V, and inductor voltage is zero.</p>\n<p><strong>E2: bypassing a source-side resistor.</strong> A 6 V source initially supplies a 2 kΩ feed resistor and two branches: <code>2 kΩ + L</code> and <code>1 kΩ + C</code>. In the pre-switch steady state, <code>i_L = 1.5 mA</code> and <code>u_C = 3 V</code>. The switch then bypasses the feed resistor. At the instant afterward, the same inductor current and capacitor voltage remain. Other variables are recalculated in the new topology; the expanded solution appears in 2.3 below.</p>\n<p><strong>E3: initially disconnected network.</strong> A source connects through R to parallel branches <code>R₁</code>, <code>R₂ + L</code>, and C. With the passive network initially unenergized, <code>u_C(0⁺)=0</code> and <code>i_L(0⁺)=0</code>. <strong>Worked here:</strong> the capacitor clamps the parallel-node voltage to zero at that instant, giving <code>i_R1(0⁺)=0</code>, <code>u_L(0⁺)=0</code>, and source/capacitor current <code>6/R</code>. These conclusions depend on the stated unenergized initial condition.</p>", "text": "  Source: 9 pages, including three example circuits.  \n Set the switching event to  t = 0 . Use  0⁻  for immediately before it and  0⁺  for immediately after it. \n  u_C(0⁺) = u_C(0⁻)  \n  i_L(0⁺) = i_L(0⁻) . \n Continuity at one instant does not mean the quantity never changes afterward. Capacitor current and inductor voltage are not subject to these continuity constraints and can jump. \n DC steady-state replacements \n Once a DC steady state is established: \n \n Capacitor: open circuit, because  du_C/dt = 0  gives  i_C = 0 . \n Inductor: short circuit, because  di_L/dt = 0  gives  u_L = 0 . \n \n These are  steady-state  replacements. At  0⁺ , retain the storage state instead of automatically replacing an inductor with a short or a capacitor with an open. \n Course examples \n  E1: combined steady-state circuit.  A 6 V source feeds a 2 Ω resistor, followed by three parallel branches: 2 Ω, 2 Ω plus L, and C. In DC steady state, L shorts and C opens. The two remaining branches give  2 Ω || 2 Ω = 1 Ω , so total current is  6/(2+1) = 2 A . Each conducting branch carries 1 A, capacitor current is zero, capacitor voltage is 2 V, and inductor voltage is zero. \n  E2: bypassing a source-side resistor.  A 6 V source initially supplies a 2 kΩ feed resistor and two branches:  2 kΩ + L  and  1 kΩ + C . In the pre-switch steady state,  i_L = 1.5 mA  and  u_C = 3 V . The switch then bypasses the feed resistor. At the instant afterward, the same inductor current and capacitor voltage remain. Other variables are recalculated in the new topology; the expanded solution appears in 2.3 below. \n  E3: initially disconnected network.  A source connects through R to parallel branches  R₁ ,  R₂ + L , and C. With the passive network initially unenergized,  u_C(0⁺)=0  and  i_L(0⁺)=0 .  Worked here:  the capacitor clamps the parallel-node voltage to zero at that instant, giving  i_R1(0⁺)=0 ,  u_L(0⁺)=0 , and source/capacitor current  6/R . These conclusions depend on the stated unenergized initial condition. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "2.3", "chapter": 2, "title": "Three-element method for first order circuit", "html": "<p><em>Source: three documents: overview, 14 pages; initial value, 16 pages; final value and time constant, 22 pages. All reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>A first-order circuit has one independent energy-storage state, or an equivalent single storage element, and a first-order differential equation. The classical method solves that equation. The three-element method obtains the same response from three quantities:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Initial value <code>f(0⁺)</code>.</li>\n<li>Final steady-state value <code>f(∞)</code>.</li>\n<li>Time constant <code>τ</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For a stable first-order linear circuit with constant post-switch excitation and fixed topology:</p>\n<p><code>f(t) = f(∞) + [f(0⁺) - f(∞)]e^(−t/τ)</code>, for <code>t ≥ 0</code>.</p>\n<p>The final value is the destination; the exponential term describes the remaining difference from that destination. Use the formula separately for each interval if switching occurs again.</p>\n<h3>Why the formula works</h3>\n<p>For a series RC circuit, KVL and the capacitor law give</p>\n<p><code>U = Ri + u_C</code>, <code>i = C du_C/dt</code>.</p>\n<p>Therefore <code>RC du_C/dt + u_C = U</code>, with solution</p>\n<p><code>u_C(t) = U + (U₀ - U)e^(−t/RC)</code>.</p>\n<p>For a series RL circuit:</p>\n<p><code>U = Ri_L + L di_L/dt</code>,</p>\n<p>so</p>\n<p><code>i_L(t) = U/R + (I₀ - U/R)e^(−tR/L)</code>.</p>\n<p>The teacher then applies Thévenin/Norton equivalence to reduce more complex first-order networks to a simple RC or RL circuit. This is the bridge between Chapters 1 and 2.</p>\n<h3>A. Finding initial values</h3>\n<p>Analyze the <strong>pre-switch</strong> circuit to find <code>u_C(0⁻)</code> and <code>i_L(0⁻)</code>. Use the switching laws to obtain their <code>0⁺</code> values. Then analyze the <strong>post-switch</strong> circuit to determine the other initial currents and voltages.</p>\n<p>At <code>0⁺</code>, a capacitor with a known voltage can be represented momentarily by that voltage constraint, and an inductor with a known current by that current constraint. These are tools for the instant-specific calculation, not replacements valid for all later times.</p>\n<p>The initial-value document contains six examples:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Circuit situation</th>\n<th>Initial result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>E1</td>\n<td>6 V source and 2 kΩ feed, an inductor, and a switched 2 kΩ shunt</td>\n<td><strong>Worked here:</strong> if initially in DC steady state, <code>i_L(0⁺) = 3 mA</code>; the shunt does not change that instantly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E2</td>\n<td>10 V source newly connected through 1 kΩ and L to a parallel 10 kΩ resistor and capacitor, with <code>u_C(0⁻)=0</code></td>\n<td><strong>Worked here:</strong> initially unenergized L has <code>i_L(0⁺)=0</code>, and KCL gives capacitor current zero at that instant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E3</td>\n<td>1 A source initially feeding <code>2 kΩ + L</code>; a 1 kΩ parallel branch closes</td>\n<td><code>i_L(0⁻)=i_L(0⁺)=1 A</code>; new branch current is initially zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E4</td>\n<td>6 V source, bypassed 2 kΩ feed, <code>2 kΩ + L</code> and <code>1 kΩ + C</code> branches</td>\n<td><code>u_C(0⁺)=3 V</code>, <code>i_L(0⁺)=1.5 mA</code>, <code>i_C(0⁺)=3 mA</code>, source current <code>4.5 mA</code>, and <code>u_L(0⁺)=3 V</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E5</td>\n<td>Initially unenergized 1 H inductor connected through 1 kΩ to 20 V</td>\n<td><code>i_L(0⁺)=0</code>; <code>u_L(0⁺)=20 V</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E6</td>\n<td>Steady 20 V, 1 kΩ, 1 H circuit interrupted, leaving a 500 kΩ voltmeter in its discharge path</td>\n<td>Current initially remains <code>20 mA</code>; meter-voltage magnitude is <code>0.020 × 500,000 = 10,000 V</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>E4 reasoning:</strong> before switching, the capacitor branch is open and the inductor is a short, giving <code>6 V/(2k+2k)=1.5 mA</code> and a 3 V node. After bypassing the feed resistor, the node jumps to 6 V while the capacitor stays at 3 V. Thus <code>i_C=(6-3)/1k=3 mA</code>; the 2 kΩ inductor branch still carries 1.5 mA and drops 3 V across its resistor, leaving 3 V across L.</p>\n<p><strong>E6 polarity clarification:</strong> the slide prints 10 kV as a positive value. With its downward meter-voltage reference retained, the inductor's continuing discharge current actually makes the top meter terminal negative, so the signed meter reading is <code>−10 kV</code>. The magnitude is 10 kV. The lesson lists wire-explosion spraying, spot welding, electronic ignition, and flash lamps as related transient applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope caution:</strong> some initial-value examples contain both L and C. Their switching laws remain valid, but that does not make the complete two-storage-element response a single first-order exponential.</p>\n<h3>B. Finding final values</h3>\n<p>Use the <strong>post-switch</strong> topology. For its final DC steady state, open capacitors and short inductors, then solve the resulting resistive network.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher E1:</strong> a 4 mA source feeds two identical conducting 3 Ω branches after the inductor shorts. The inductor-branch final current is <code>2 mA</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher E2:</strong> two 4 kΩ resistors connect in parallel between the 10 V source's top node and the capacitor node; a 3 kΩ resistor connects that node to the bottom rail. The capacitor opens at infinity, giving</p>\n<p><code>u_C(∞) = 10 × 3/(4||4 + 3) = 10 × 3/5 = 6 V</code>.</p>\n<h3>C. Finding time constants</h3>\n<p><code>τ_RC = R_seen C</code>, <code>τ_RL = L/R_seen</code>.</p>\n<p>Find <code>R_seen</code> looking into the storage element's terminals in the <strong>post-switch</strong> circuit. Suppress independent sources: voltage sources become shorts and current sources become opens. Keep the circuit's relevant resistances.</p>\n<p>A larger τ means a slower response. At one τ, a charging waveform has completed 63.2% of its initial-to-final change; the remaining error is 36.8%.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Remaining initial-to-final difference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>τ</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">36.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>2τ</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">13.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>3τ</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5.0%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>4τ</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>5τ</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.67%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slides call <code>t ≥ 3τ</code> approximately settled. That corresponds to accepting about 5% remaining error; 5τ is a stricter approximation. Neither is an exact endpoint.</p>\n<p><strong>RL time-constant example:</strong> opening the suppressed current source removes its series 2 Ω feed branch from the resistance seen by the inductor. The two remaining 3 Ω resistors form 6 Ω in the discharge path. With <code>L = 1 H</code>, <code>τ = 1/6 s</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>RC time-constant example:</strong> suppressing the 10 V source makes two upper 6 kΩ resistors and the lower 3 kΩ resistor all appear in parallel from the capacitor terminals. Thus</p>\n<p><code>R_seen = 6k || 6k || 3k = 1.5 kΩ</code>, and <code>τ = 1500 C</code> seconds when C is in farads.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide correction:</strong> the printed answer says 1.5 Ω, but the resistor labels require <strong>1.5 kΩ</strong>. No numerical capacitance is provided in that example.</p>\n<p><strong>Final bridge review, worked here:</strong> an 18 V supply feeds two dividers: <code>3 kΩ over 6 kΩ</code> on the left and <code>6 kΩ over 3 kΩ</code> on the right, with C between their midpoints. At infinity, the midpoint voltages are 12 V and 6 V, so capacitor voltage is 6 V with the left plate positive. With the source shorted, <code>R_seen = (3k||6k) + (6k||3k) = 4 kΩ</code>, so <code>τ = 4000 C</code> seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Units:</strong> <code>1 F = 10⁶ µF = 10¹² pF</code>; <code>kΩ × µF = ms</code>.</p>", "text": "  Source: three documents: overview, 14 pages; initial value, 16 pages; final value and time constant, 22 pages. All reviewed.  \n A first-order circuit has one independent energy-storage state, or an equivalent single storage element, and a first-order differential equation. The classical method solves that equation. The three-element method obtains the same response from three quantities: \n \n Initial value  f(0⁺) . \n Final steady-state value  f(∞) . \n Time constant  τ . \n \n For a stable first-order linear circuit with constant post-switch excitation and fixed topology: \n  f(t) = f(∞) + [f(0⁺) - f(∞)]e^(−t/τ) , for  t ≥ 0 . \n The final value is the destination; the exponential term describes the remaining difference from that destination. Use the formula separately for each interval if switching occurs again. \n Why the formula works \n For a series RC circuit, KVL and the capacitor law give \n  U = Ri + u_C ,  i = C du_C/dt . \n Therefore  RC du_C/dt + u_C = U , with solution \n  u_C(t) = U + (U₀ - U)e^(−t/RC) . \n For a series RL circuit: \n  U = Ri_L + L di_L/dt , \n so \n  i_L(t) = U/R + (I₀ - U/R)e^(−tR/L) . \n The teacher then applies Thévenin/Norton equivalence to reduce more complex first-order networks to a simple RC or RL circuit. This is the bridge between Chapters 1 and 2. \n A. Finding initial values \n Analyze the  pre-switch  circuit to find  u_C(0⁻)  and  i_L(0⁻) . Use the switching laws to obtain their  0⁺  values. Then analyze the  post-switch  circuit to determine the other initial currents and voltages. \n At  0⁺ , a capacitor with a known voltage can be represented momentarily by that voltage constraint, and an inductor with a known current by that current constraint. These are tools for the instant-specific calculation, not replacements valid for all later times. \n The initial-value document contains six examples: \n \n \n \n Example \n Circuit situation \n Initial result \n \n \n \n \n E1 \n 6 V source and 2 kΩ feed, an inductor, and a switched 2 kΩ shunt \n  Worked here:  if initially in DC steady state,  i_L(0⁺) = 3 mA ; the shunt does not change that instantly \n \n \n E2 \n 10 V source newly connected through 1 kΩ and L to a parallel 10 kΩ resistor and capacitor, with  u_C(0⁻)=0  \n  Worked here:  initially unenergized L has  i_L(0⁺)=0 , and KCL gives capacitor current zero at that instant \n \n \n E3 \n 1 A source initially feeding  2 kΩ + L ; a 1 kΩ parallel branch closes \n  i_L(0⁻)=i_L(0⁺)=1 A ; new branch current is initially zero \n \n \n E4 \n 6 V source, bypassed 2 kΩ feed,  2 kΩ + L  and  1 kΩ + C  branches \n  u_C(0⁺)=3 V ,  i_L(0⁺)=1.5 mA ,  i_C(0⁺)=3 mA , source current  4.5 mA , and  u_L(0⁺)=3 V  \n \n \n E5 \n Initially unenergized 1 H inductor connected through 1 kΩ to 20 V \n  i_L(0⁺)=0 ;  u_L(0⁺)=20 V  \n \n \n E6 \n Steady 20 V, 1 kΩ, 1 H circuit interrupted, leaving a 500 kΩ voltmeter in its discharge path \n Current initially remains  20 mA ; meter-voltage magnitude is  0.020 × 500,000 = 10,000 V  \n \n \n \n  E4 reasoning:  before switching, the capacitor branch is open and the inductor is a short, giving  6 V/(2k+2k)=1.5 mA  and a 3 V node. After bypassing the feed resistor, the node jumps to 6 V while the capacitor stays at 3 V. Thus  i_C=(6-3)/1k=3 mA ; the 2 kΩ inductor branch still carries 1.5 mA and drops 3 V across its resistor, leaving 3 V across L. \n  E6 polarity clarification:  the slide prints 10 kV as a positive value. With its downward meter-voltage reference retained, the inductor's continuing discharge current actually makes the top meter terminal negative, so the signed meter reading is  −10 kV . The magnitude is 10 kV. The lesson lists wire-explosion spraying, spot welding, electronic ignition, and flash lamps as related transient applications. \n  Scope caution:  some initial-value examples contain both L and C. Their switching laws remain valid, but that does not make the complete two-storage-element response a single first-order exponential. \n B. Finding final values \n Use the  post-switch  topology. For its final DC steady state, open capacitors and short inductors, then solve the resulting resistive network. \n  Teacher E1:  a 4 mA source feeds two identical conducting 3 Ω branches after the inductor shorts. The inductor-branch final current is  2 mA . \n  Teacher E2:  two 4 kΩ resistors connect in parallel between the 10 V source's top node and the capacitor node; a 3 kΩ resistor connects that node to the bottom rail. The capacitor opens at infinity, giving \n  u_C(∞) = 10 × 3/(4||4 + 3) = 10 × 3/5 = 6 V . \n C. Finding time constants \n  τ_RC = R_seen C ,  τ_RL = L/R_seen . \n Find  R_seen  looking into the storage element's terminals in the  post-switch  circuit. Suppress independent sources: voltage sources become shorts and current sources become opens. Keep the circuit's relevant resistances. \n A larger τ means a slower response. At one τ, a charging waveform has completed 63.2% of its initial-to-final change; the remaining error is 36.8%. \n \n \n \n Time \n Remaining initial-to-final difference \n \n \n \n \n  τ  \n 36.8% \n \n \n  2τ  \n 13.5% \n \n \n  3τ  \n 5.0% \n \n \n  4τ  \n 1.8% \n \n \n  5τ  \n 0.67% \n \n \n \n The slides call  t ≥ 3τ  approximately settled. That corresponds to accepting about 5% remaining error; 5τ is a stricter approximation. Neither is an exact endpoint. \n  RL time-constant example:  opening the suppressed current source removes its series 2 Ω feed branch from the resistance seen by the inductor. The two remaining 3 Ω resistors form 6 Ω in the discharge path. With  L = 1 H ,  τ = 1/6 s . \n  RC time-constant example:  suppressing the 10 V source makes two upper 6 kΩ resistors and the lower 3 kΩ resistor all appear in parallel from the capacitor terminals. Thus \n  R_seen = 6k || 6k || 3k = 1.5 kΩ , and  τ = 1500 C  seconds when C is in farads. \n  Slide correction:  the printed answer says 1.5 Ω, but the resistor labels require  1.5 kΩ . No numerical capacitance is provided in that example. \n  Final bridge review, worked here:  an 18 V supply feeds two dividers:  3 kΩ over 6 kΩ  on the left and  6 kΩ over 3 kΩ  on the right, with C between their midpoints. At infinity, the midpoint voltages are 12 V and 6 V, so capacitor voltage is 6 V with the left plate positive. With the source shorted,  R_seen = (3k||6k) + (6k||3k) = 4 kΩ , so  τ = 4000 C  seconds. \n  Units:   1 F = 10⁶ µF = 10¹² pF ;  kΩ × µF = ms . ", "minutes": 6}, {"id": "2.4", "chapter": 2, "title": "Transient Analysis for RC Circuit", "html": "<p><em>Source: 14 pages; pp. 1-10 cover RC response types, and pp. 11-14 contain the embedded RL material.</em></p>\n<h3>Zero-state response</h3>\n<p>There is no initial stored energy; the source creates the response. For an initially uncharged capacitor connected through R to <code>U_s</code>:</p>\n<p><code>u_C(0⁺)=0</code>, <code>i_C(0⁺)=U_s/R</code>, <code>u_C(∞)=U_s</code>, <code>i_C(∞)=0</code>, <code>τ=RC</code>.</p>\n<p>Therefore</p>\n<p><code>u_C(t) = U_s(1-e^(−t/RC))</code></p>\n<p><code>i_C(t) = (U_s/R)e^(−t/RC)</code>.</p>\n<p>Voltage rises continuously; current starts at its maximum and decays. The current formula also follows by differentiating the voltage: <code>i_C=C du_C/dt</code>.</p>\n<h3>Zero-input response</h3>\n<p>Disconnect the source and allow an initially charged capacitor of voltage <code>U₀</code> to discharge through R:</p>\n<p><code>u_C(t) = U₀e^(−t/RC)</code>.</p>\n<p>For current referenced <strong>into the capacitor's positive terminal</strong>:</p>\n<p><code>i_C(t) = -(U₀/R)e^(−t/RC)</code>.</p>\n<p>The negative sign means actual discharge current leaves the positive plate. The voltage and stored energy decrease, while energy is dissipated in R.</p>\n<h3>Complete response</h3>\n<p>The source and initial stored energy both contribute. The slides switch a capacitor, initially settled at <code>U_s1</code>, to a second source <code>U_s2</code> through R:</p>\n<p><code>u_C(t) = U_s2 + (U_s1-U_s2)e^(−t/RC)</code></p>\n<p><code>i_C(t) = [(U_s2-U_s1)/R]e^(−t/RC)</code>.</p>\n<p>If <code>U_s2 &gt; U_s1</code>, voltage rises; if <code>U_s2 &lt; U_s1</code>, it falls. The same formula covers either case.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide correction:</strong> the small initial/final-value table beside the complete-response example repeats values from the zero-state example. The displayed main equation correctly requires <code>u_C(0⁺)=U_s1</code> and <code>i_C(0⁺)=(U_s2-U_s1)/R</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Useful consequence, worked here:</strong> to find the time to reach a target strictly between the initial and final values, rearrange the exponential:</p>\n<p><code>t = -τ ln[(u_target-u_final)/(u_initial-u_final)]</code>.</p>\n<p>Check that the ratio is positive and the target lies on the waveform's path.</p>", "text": "  Source: 14 pages; pp. 1-10 cover RC response types, and pp. 11-14 contain the embedded RL material.  \n Zero-state response \n There is no initial stored energy; the source creates the response. For an initially uncharged capacitor connected through R to  U_s : \n  u_C(0⁺)=0 ,  i_C(0⁺)=U_s/R ,  u_C(∞)=U_s ,  i_C(∞)=0 ,  τ=RC . \n Therefore \n  u_C(t) = U_s(1-e^(−t/RC))  \n  i_C(t) = (U_s/R)e^(−t/RC) . \n Voltage rises continuously; current starts at its maximum and decays. The current formula also follows by differentiating the voltage:  i_C=C du_C/dt . \n Zero-input response \n Disconnect the source and allow an initially charged capacitor of voltage  U₀  to discharge through R: \n  u_C(t) = U₀e^(−t/RC) . \n For current referenced  into the capacitor's positive terminal : \n  i_C(t) = -(U₀/R)e^(−t/RC) . \n The negative sign means actual discharge current leaves the positive plate. The voltage and stored energy decrease, while energy is dissipated in R. \n Complete response \n The source and initial stored energy both contribute. The slides switch a capacitor, initially settled at  U_s1 , to a second source  U_s2  through R: \n  u_C(t) = U_s2 + (U_s1-U_s2)e^(−t/RC)  \n  i_C(t) = [(U_s2-U_s1)/R]e^(−t/RC) . \n If  U_s2 > U_s1 , voltage rises; if  U_s2 < U_s1 , it falls. The same formula covers either case. \n  Slide correction:  the small initial/final-value table beside the complete-response example repeats values from the zero-state example. The displayed main equation correctly requires  u_C(0⁺)=U_s1  and  i_C(0⁺)=(U_s2-U_s1)/R . \n  Useful consequence, worked here:  to find the time to reach a target strictly between the initial and final values, rearrange the exponential: \n  t = -τ ln[(u_target-u_final)/(u_initial-u_final)] . \n Check that the ratio is positive and the target lies on the waveform's path. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "2.5", "chapter": 2, "title": "Transient Analysis for RL Circuit", "html": "<p><img alt=\"Original course slide, lesson 2.4 document, page 14: the embedded RL decay example.\" src=\"assets/rl-decay-example.png\" /></p>\n<p><em>Original course slide, lesson 2.4 document, page 14: the embedded RL decay example.</em></p>\n<p>This material is in the 2.4 document and is part of the reviewed theory coverage even though it has no separate course-index entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example:</strong> a 3 A source initially feeds a 2 Ω series resistor and then parallel branches of 2 Ω and <code>1 Ω + 1 H</code>. At pre-switch DC steady state, the inductor shorts. Current division gives <code>i_L(0⁻)=2 A</code>.</p>\n<p>The switch then disconnects the current source and grounds the left end of the feed resistor, leaving a discharge network.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial current: <code>i_L(0⁺)=2 A</code>.</li>\n<li>Final current: <code>i_L(∞)=0</code>.</li>\n<li>Resistance seen by L: <code>R_seen=1+(2||2)=2 Ω</code>.</li>\n<li>Time constant: <code>τ=1/2=0.5 s</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence</p>\n<p><code>i_L(t)=2e^(−2t) A</code>.</p>\n<p>The inductor voltage, with the marked downward passive reference, is</p>\n<p><code>u_L(t)=L di_L/dt=-4e^(−2t) V</code>.</p>\n<p>The current remains positive while its derivative and voltage are negative: the inductor releases its stored energy into the resistors.</p>", "text": "   \n  Original course slide, lesson 2.4 document, page 14: the embedded RL decay example.  \n This material is in the 2.4 document and is part of the reviewed theory coverage even though it has no separate course-index entry. \n  Teacher example:  a 3 A source initially feeds a 2 Ω series resistor and then parallel branches of 2 Ω and  1 Ω + 1 H . At pre-switch DC steady state, the inductor shorts. Current division gives  i_L(0⁻)=2 A . \n The switch then disconnects the current source and grounds the left end of the feed resistor, leaving a discharge network. \n \n Initial current:  i_L(0⁺)=2 A . \n Final current:  i_L(∞)=0 . \n Resistance seen by L:  R_seen=1+(2||2)=2 Ω . \n Time constant:  τ=1/2=0.5 s . \n \n Hence \n  i_L(t)=2e^(−2t) A . \n The inductor voltage, with the marked downward passive reference, is \n  u_L(t)=L di_L/dt=-4e^(−2t) V . \n The current remains positive while its derivative and voltage are negative: the inductor releases its stored energy into the resistors. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "chapter": 3, "title": "Basic concept", "html": "<p><em>Source: 13 pages, all reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>The alternator drawings introduce a rotating coil in a magnetic field. As its orientation changes, the induced voltage varies and reverses polarity. AC is voltage that reverses polarity or current that reverses direction over time; a changing magnitude alone is insufficient.</p>\n<p>In the waveform-identification question, the bipolar triangular, square, and sinusoidal waveforms are AC. The positive-only pulse train and rectified waveform are unidirectional pulsating signals, not AC in the classification used by this lesson.</p>\n<h3>Three defining parameters of a sinusoid</h3>\n<p>The teacher writes</p>\n<p><code>i(t)=I_m sin(ωt+ψ)</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Unit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>i(t)</code></td>\n<td>Instantaneous current</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>I_m</code></td>\n<td>Peak amplitude</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ω</code></td>\n<td>Angular frequency</td>\n<td>rad/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ψ</code></td>\n<td>Initial phase</td>\n<td>radians or degrees, consistently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>f</code></td>\n<td>Cycles per second</td>\n<td>Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>T</code></td>\n<td>Period</td>\n<td>s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>T=1/f</code> and <code>ω=2πf=2π/T</code>.</p>\n<p>The teacher's waveform example has <code>f=2 Hz</code>, <code>T=0.5 s</code>, and <code>ω=4π rad/s</code>. Frequency, period, and angular frequency express the same repetition rate in different forms.</p>\n<h3>Instantaneous, peak, and effective values</h3>\n<p>The course uses lower-case <code>e, i, u</code> for instantaneous values; <code>E_m, I_m, U_m</code> for amplitudes; and <code>E, I, U</code> for effective or RMS values.</p>\n<p>For pure sinusoids:</p>\n<p><code>I=I_m/√2</code>, <code>U=U_m/√2</code>, <code>E=E_m/√2</code>.</p>\n<p>RMS represents the equivalent heating effect in a resistor. A complete-cycle signed sine average is zero, but its RMS value and heating effect are not zero. The familiar 220 V and 380 V sinusoidal supply ratings are RMS values. Ordinary AC-meter readings are interpreted as RMS for the intended sinusoidal operating conditions; an oscilloscope shows the instantaneous waveform, from which peak values can be measured.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher's heater question, worked here as an ideal-resistor calculation:</strong> a constant-resistance heater rated at 300 V would dissipate <code>(220/300)²≈0.538</code> of its rated power at 220 V RMS. Comparing only the AC peak with the DC rating gives the wrong heating comparison. This calculation does not establish the suitability of a particular appliance.</p>\n<h3>Phase and phase difference</h3>\n<p>Phase is the full angle <code>ωt+ψ</code>; initial phase is its value at <code>t=0</code>. A fixed phase difference is meaningful when comparing same-frequency sinusoids expressed with the same sine/cosine reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>i=10 sin(1000t+30°) A</code>: initial phase <code>+30°</code>.</li>\n<li><code>u=311 sin(314t-60°) V</code>: initial phase <code>-60°</code>.</li>\n<li>With <code>i=100 sin(314t+30°)</code> and the above voltage, <code>φ=ψ_u-ψ_i=-60°-30°=-90°</code>. Voltage lags current by 90°, or current leads voltage by 90°.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Strictly, convert angular terms to a consistent unit when evaluating a waveform numerically; a notation mixing <code>ωt</code> and a degree-marked offset is shorthand for that conversion.</p>\n<p>For <code>φ=ψ_u-ψ_i</code>: positive φ means voltage leads current; negative φ means voltage lags current. Zero means in phase; ±180° means opposite phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Questions present but not answered in the slides:</strong> why supply systems use 50/60 Hz, what frequency an electronic fluorescent ballast uses, and advantages of high-frequency operation. Retain these as discussion topics; no single numerical ballast frequency is supplied in the reviewed material.</p>", "text": "  Source: 13 pages, all reviewed.  \n The alternator drawings introduce a rotating coil in a magnetic field. As its orientation changes, the induced voltage varies and reverses polarity. AC is voltage that reverses polarity or current that reverses direction over time; a changing magnitude alone is insufficient. \n In the waveform-identification question, the bipolar triangular, square, and sinusoidal waveforms are AC. The positive-only pulse train and rectified waveform are unidirectional pulsating signals, not AC in the classification used by this lesson. \n Three defining parameters of a sinusoid \n The teacher writes \n  i(t)=I_m sin(ωt+ψ) . \n \n \n \n Symbol \n Meaning \n Unit \n \n \n \n \n  i(t)  \n Instantaneous current \n A \n \n \n  I_m  \n Peak amplitude \n A \n \n \n  ω  \n Angular frequency \n rad/s \n \n \n  ψ  \n Initial phase \n radians or degrees, consistently \n \n \n  f  \n Cycles per second \n Hz \n \n \n  T  \n Period \n s \n \n \n \n  T=1/f  and  ω=2πf=2π/T . \n The teacher's waveform example has  f=2 Hz ,  T=0.5 s , and  ω=4π rad/s . Frequency, period, and angular frequency express the same repetition rate in different forms. \n Instantaneous, peak, and effective values \n The course uses lower-case  e, i, u  for instantaneous values;  E_m, I_m, U_m  for amplitudes; and  E, I, U  for effective or RMS values. \n For pure sinusoids: \n  I=I_m/√2 ,  U=U_m/√2 ,  E=E_m/√2 . \n RMS represents the equivalent heating effect in a resistor. A complete-cycle signed sine average is zero, but its RMS value and heating effect are not zero. The familiar 220 V and 380 V sinusoidal supply ratings are RMS values. Ordinary AC-meter readings are interpreted as RMS for the intended sinusoidal operating conditions; an oscilloscope shows the instantaneous waveform, from which peak values can be measured. \n  Teacher's heater question, worked here as an ideal-resistor calculation:  a constant-resistance heater rated at 300 V would dissipate  (220/300)²≈0.538  of its rated power at 220 V RMS. Comparing only the AC peak with the DC rating gives the wrong heating comparison. This calculation does not establish the suitability of a particular appliance. \n Phase and phase difference \n Phase is the full angle  ωt+ψ ; initial phase is its value at  t=0 . A fixed phase difference is meaningful when comparing same-frequency sinusoids expressed with the same sine/cosine reference. \n  Teacher examples:  \n \n  i=10 sin(1000t+30°) A : initial phase  +30° . \n  u=311 sin(314t-60°) V : initial phase  -60° . \n With  i=100 sin(314t+30°)  and the above voltage,  φ=ψ_u-ψ_i=-60°-30°=-90° . Voltage lags current by 90°, or current leads voltage by 90°. \n \n Strictly, convert angular terms to a consistent unit when evaluating a waveform numerically; a notation mixing  ωt  and a degree-marked offset is shorthand for that conversion. \n For  φ=ψ_u-ψ_i : positive φ means voltage leads current; negative φ means voltage lags current. Zero means in phase; ±180° means opposite phase. \n  Questions present but not answered in the slides:  why supply systems use 50/60 Hz, what frequency an electronic fluorescent ballast uses, and advantages of high-frequency operation. Retain these as discussion topics; no single numerical ballast frequency is supplied in the reviewed material. ", "minutes": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "chapter": 3, "title": "Phasor representation", "html": "<p><em>Sources: rotating-vector/complex-number document, 10 pages; sinusoidal-AC phasor document, 7 pages.</em></p>\n<h3>From sine wave to phasor</h3>\n<p>The y-component of an anticlockwise rotating vector produces a sine wave. At one frequency, the relative positions of the vectors stay fixed; representing their magnitudes and initial angles gives phasors that are easier to add and divide than time-varying sine expressions.</p>\n<p>A phasor contains magnitude and phase. Its frequency is part of the shared problem context and is not encoded in the phasor alone.</p>\n<h3>Four forms of a complex number</h3>\n<p><code>a+jb = M(cosψ+j sinψ) = M e^(jψ) = M∠ψ</code>, where <code>j²=-1</code>.</p>\n<p>These are rectangular, trigonometric, exponential, and polar forms. The slide calls the trigonometric form “triangular”; the formulas show what is meant.</p>\n<p><code>M=√(a²+b²)</code>, <code>a=M cosψ</code>, <code>b=M sinψ</code>. Use the correct quadrant when finding ψ; <code>atan2(b,a)</code> handles this reliably.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Convenient form</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Add/subtract</td>\n<td>Rectangular</td>\n<td>Combine real and imaginary parts separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiply</td>\n<td>Polar</td>\n<td>Multiply magnitudes; add angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Divide</td>\n<td>Polar</td>\n<td>Divide magnitudes; subtract angles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Teacher examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>1+j=√2∠45°</code>; <code>j=1∠90°</code>.</li>\n<li><code>(1+j)+(5+j6)=6+j7</code>.</li>\n<li><code>(1+j)/(√3/2+j/2)=√2∠15°</code>.</li>\n<li><code>(1+j)(1/2+j√3/2)/(√3/2+j/2)=√2∠75°</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Multiplication by <code>j=e^(j90°)</code> rotates a phasor anticlockwise by 90°. Multiplication by <code>-j=e^(−j90°)</code> rotates it clockwise by 90°. Neither changes magnitude.</p>\n<h3>RMS versus peak phasors</h3>\n<p>Using the course's sine reference:</p>\n<p><code>i(t)=√2 I sin(ωt+ψ)</code> corresponds to RMS phasor <code>Ĩ=I∠ψ</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>20√2 sin(1000t+30°)</code> has RMS phasor <code>20∠30° A</code> and peak phasor <code>20√2∠30° A</code>. In contrast, <code>20 sin(1000t+30°)</code> has RMS phasor <code>(20/√2)∠30° A</code>.</p>\n<p>The teacher marks the attempted conversion from <code>Ĩ=20∠30°</code> directly to <code>20 sin(1000t+30°)</code> as wrong: the RMS/peak factor is missing, and the phasor alone did not specify 1000 rad/s. If ω is supplied elsewhere, the correct waveform is <code>20√2 sin(ωt+30°)</code>.</p>\n<h3>Teacher's current-addition example</h3>\n<p><code>i₁=12.7√2 sin(314t+30°) A</code></p>\n<p><code>i₂=11√2 sin(314t-60°) A</code>.</p>\n<p>Convert to RMS phasors:</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ₁=12.7∠30°</code>, <code>Ĩ₂=11∠-60°</code>.</p>\n<p>Add in rectangular form:</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ≈(11.00+j6.35)+(5.50-j9.526)=16.499-j3.176 A</code>.</p>\n<p>Therefore</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ≈16.8∠-10.9° A</code></p>\n<p><code>i(t)≈16.8√2 sin(314t-10.9°) A</code>.</p>\n<p>The RMS current is about 16.8 A, not 23.7 A. These two phasors can be added directly because their frequency is the same.</p>", "text": "  Sources: rotating-vector/complex-number document, 10 pages; sinusoidal-AC phasor document, 7 pages.  \n From sine wave to phasor \n The y-component of an anticlockwise rotating vector produces a sine wave. At one frequency, the relative positions of the vectors stay fixed; representing their magnitudes and initial angles gives phasors that are easier to add and divide than time-varying sine expressions. \n A phasor contains magnitude and phase. Its frequency is part of the shared problem context and is not encoded in the phasor alone. \n Four forms of a complex number \n  a+jb = M(cosψ+j sinψ) = M e^(jψ) = M∠ψ , where  j²=-1 . \n These are rectangular, trigonometric, exponential, and polar forms. The slide calls the trigonometric form “triangular”; the formulas show what is meant. \n  M=√(a²+b²) ,  a=M cosψ ,  b=M sinψ . Use the correct quadrant when finding ψ;  atan2(b,a)  handles this reliably. \n \n \n \n Operation \n Convenient form \n Rule \n \n \n \n \n Add/subtract \n Rectangular \n Combine real and imaginary parts separately \n \n \n Multiply \n Polar \n Multiply magnitudes; add angles \n \n \n Divide \n Polar \n Divide magnitudes; subtract angles \n \n \n \n  Teacher examples:  \n \n  1+j=√2∠45° ;  j=1∠90° . \n  (1+j)+(5+j6)=6+j7 . \n  (1+j)/(√3/2+j/2)=√2∠15° . \n  (1+j)(1/2+j√3/2)/(√3/2+j/2)=√2∠75° . \n \n Multiplication by  j=e^(j90°)  rotates a phasor anticlockwise by 90°. Multiplication by  -j=e^(−j90°)  rotates it clockwise by 90°. Neither changes magnitude. \n RMS versus peak phasors \n Using the course's sine reference: \n  i(t)=√2 I sin(ωt+ψ)  corresponds to RMS phasor  Ĩ=I∠ψ . \n For example,  20√2 sin(1000t+30°)  has RMS phasor  20∠30° A  and peak phasor  20√2∠30° A . In contrast,  20 sin(1000t+30°)  has RMS phasor  (20/√2)∠30° A . \n The teacher marks the attempted conversion from  Ĩ=20∠30°  directly to  20 sin(1000t+30°)  as wrong: the RMS/peak factor is missing, and the phasor alone did not specify 1000 rad/s. If ω is supplied elsewhere, the correct waveform is  20√2 sin(ωt+30°) . \n Teacher's current-addition example \n  i₁=12.7√2 sin(314t+30°) A  \n  i₂=11√2 sin(314t-60°) A . \n Convert to RMS phasors: \n  Ĩ₁=12.7∠30° ,  Ĩ₂=11∠-60° . \n Add in rectangular form: \n  Ĩ≈(11.00+j6.35)+(5.50-j9.526)=16.499-j3.176 A . \n Therefore \n  Ĩ≈16.8∠-10.9° A  \n  i(t)≈16.8√2 sin(314t-10.9°) A . \n The RMS current is about 16.8 A, not 23.7 A. These two phasors can be added directly because their frequency is the same. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "3.3", "chapter": 3, "title": "Single parameter AC circuit", "html": "<p><em>Source: 6 pages, all reviewed.</em></p>\n<p>The lesson compares time waveforms, phasor diagrams, effective-value relationships, and phasor equations for ideal R, L, and C.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>RMS magnitude relation</th>\n<th>Phasor equation</th>\n<th>Phase relationship</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Resistor</td>\n<td><code>U=RI</code></td>\n<td><code>Ũ=RĨ</code></td>\n<td>Voltage and current in phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inductor</td>\n<td><code>U=X_L I</code>, <code>X_L=ωL=2πfL</code></td>\n<td><code>Ũ=jX_LĨ</code></td>\n<td>Voltage leads current by 90°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitor</td>\n<td><code>U=X_C I</code>, <code>X_C=1/(ωC)=1/(2πfC)</code></td>\n<td><code>Ũ=-jX_CĨ</code></td>\n<td>Current leads voltage by 90°</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>With <code>i=I_m sinωt</code>, the inductor law gives <code>u_L=ωLI_m cosωt</code>, explaining the voltage lead. With <code>u_C=U_m sinωt</code>, the capacitor law gives <code>i_C=ωCU_m cosωt</code>, explaining the current lead.</p>\n<p>Inductive reactance grows with frequency. Capacitive reactance magnitude decreases with frequency. <code>X_C</code> in this table is a positive magnitude; the capacitor impedance is negative imaginary.</p>\n<p><strong>Corrections to the actual slides:</strong> the capacitor caption incorrectly says voltage leads current; its waveform and phasor diagram show current leading. Its phasor equation, repeated in the summary, also prints <code>X_L</code> instead of <code>X_C</code>. Use <code>Ũ=-jX_CĨ</code>.</p>", "text": "  Source: 6 pages, all reviewed.  \n The lesson compares time waveforms, phasor diagrams, effective-value relationships, and phasor equations for ideal R, L, and C. \n \n \n \n Element \n RMS magnitude relation \n Phasor equation \n Phase relationship \n \n \n \n \n Resistor \n  U=RI  \n  Ũ=RĨ  \n Voltage and current in phase \n \n \n Inductor \n  U=X_L I ,  X_L=ωL=2πfL  \n  Ũ=jX_LĨ  \n Voltage leads current by 90° \n \n \n Capacitor \n  U=X_C I ,  X_C=1/(ωC)=1/(2πfC)  \n  Ũ=-jX_CĨ  \n Current leads voltage by 90° \n \n \n \n With  i=I_m sinωt , the inductor law gives  u_L=ωLI_m cosωt , explaining the voltage lead. With  u_C=U_m sinωt , the capacitor law gives  i_C=ωCU_m cosωt , explaining the current lead. \n Inductive reactance grows with frequency. Capacitive reactance magnitude decreases with frequency.  X_C  in this table is a positive magnitude; the capacitor impedance is negative imaginary. \n  Corrections to the actual slides:  the capacitor caption incorrectly says voltage leads current; its waveform and phasor diagram show current leading. Its phasor equation, repeated in the summary, also prints  X_L  instead of  X_C . Use  Ũ=-jX_CĨ . ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "3.4", "chapter": 3, "title": "Series AC circuit", "html": "<p><em>Sources: series RLC, 11 pages; series impedance, 4 pages.</em></p>\n<h3>Series RLC</h3>\n<p>The same current passes through all series elements. KVL holds for instantaneous voltages and for their corresponding phasors:</p>\n<p><code>Ũ=Ũ_R+Ũ_L+Ũ_C=[R+j(X_L-X_C)]Ĩ</code>.</p>\n<p>Define</p>\n<p><code>Z=R+jX</code>, with <code>X=X_L-X_C</code>.</p>\n<p><code>|Z|=√(R²+X²)</code>, <code>φ=argZ</code>, and <code>Ĩ=Ũ/Z</code>.</p>\n<p>For positive R, <code>φ=arctan(X/R)</code>. For general complex numbers, retain the correct quadrant.</p>\n<p><strong>Phasor versus magnitude:</strong> <code>Ũ=ZĨ</code> contains magnitude and phase; <code>U=|Z|I</code> contains magnitudes only. Do not write a complex impedance in a scalar-magnitude equation as though it were a real resistance.</p>\n<h3>The opening 1 V example</h3>\n<p>The slides ask for total voltage when each element has RMS magnitude <code>U_R=U_L=U_C=1 V</code>. Refer current to 0°:</p>\n<p><code>Ũ_R=1</code>, <code>Ũ_L=j</code>, <code>Ũ_C=-j</code>.</p>\n<p>Thus <code>Ũ=1+j-j=1 V</code>. The total is <strong>1 V, not 3 V</strong>. Inductive and capacitive voltage phasors cancel.</p>\n<h3>Circuit classification and impedance triangle</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Net impedance</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>X_L&gt;X_C</code></td>\n<td>Positive imaginary part</td>\n<td>Inductive; current lags voltage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>X_L&lt;X_C</code></td>\n<td>Negative imaginary part</td>\n<td>Capacitive; current leads voltage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>X_L=X_C</code></td>\n<td><code>Z=R</code></td>\n<td>Resistive terminal behavior; voltage/current in phase</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The impedance triangle has horizontal side R, signed vertical side X, and hypotenuse <code>|Z|</code>. It lets you connect resistance, reactance, magnitude, and angle.</p>\n<p>The slide's special cases are R alone, L alone, C alone, RL, RC, and LC. Their impedances follow by omitting absent elements:</p>\n<p><code>R</code>, <code>jX_L</code>, <code>-jX_C</code>, <code>R+jX_L</code>, <code>R-jX_C</code>, and <code>j(X_L-X_C)</code>.</p>\n<h3>Teacher's impedance exercises, worked here</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Given</th>\n<th>Derived R and X</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Z=300+j500 Ω</code></td>\n<td><code>R=300 Ω</code>, <code>X=500 Ω</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ũ=50∠30° V</code>, <code>Ĩ=2∠30° A</code></td>\n<td><code>Z=25 Ω</code>, so <code>R=25 Ω</code>, <code>X=0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ũ=100∠-30° V</code>, <code>Ĩ=4∠40° A</code></td>\n<td><code>Z=25∠-70° Ω</code>, so <code>R≈8.55 Ω</code>, <code>X≈-23.49 Ω</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>General series impedances</h3>\n<p>Group an RL section as <code>Z₁=R₁+jX_L</code> and an RC section as <code>Z₂=R₂-jX_C</code>. Then</p>\n<p><code>Z_total=Z₁+Z₂</code>, <code>Ĩ=Ũ/(Z₁+Z₂)</code>.</p>\n<p>The phasor voltage-divider relationships are</p>\n<p><code>Ũ₁=[Z₁/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ũ</code></p>\n<p><code>Ũ₂=[Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ũ</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher example, worked here:</strong> <code>Ũ_s=10∠0° V</code>, <code>Z₁=3+j2 Ω</code>, <code>Z₂=2+j3 Ω</code>.</p>\n<p><code>Z_total=5+j5 Ω</code>, so <code>Ĩ=10/(5+j5)=1-j1=√2∠-45° A</code>.</p>\n<p>The section voltages are <code>Ũ₁=5-j1 V</code> and <code>Ũ₂=5+j1 V</code>; their sum is the supplied 10 V. Their magnitudes should not be added arithmetically.</p>", "text": "  Sources: series RLC, 11 pages; series impedance, 4 pages.  \n Series RLC \n The same current passes through all series elements. KVL holds for instantaneous voltages and for their corresponding phasors: \n  Ũ=Ũ_R+Ũ_L+Ũ_C=[R+j(X_L-X_C)]Ĩ . \n Define \n  Z=R+jX , with  X=X_L-X_C . \n  |Z|=√(R²+X²) ,  φ=argZ , and  Ĩ=Ũ/Z . \n For positive R,  φ=arctan(X/R) . For general complex numbers, retain the correct quadrant. \n  Phasor versus magnitude:   Ũ=ZĨ  contains magnitude and phase;  U=|Z|I  contains magnitudes only. Do not write a complex impedance in a scalar-magnitude equation as though it were a real resistance. \n The opening 1 V example \n The slides ask for total voltage when each element has RMS magnitude  U_R=U_L=U_C=1 V . Refer current to 0°: \n  Ũ_R=1 ,  Ũ_L=j ,  Ũ_C=-j . \n Thus  Ũ=1+j-j=1 V . The total is  1 V, not 3 V . Inductive and capacitive voltage phasors cancel. \n Circuit classification and impedance triangle \n \n \n \n Condition \n Net impedance \n Behavior \n \n \n \n \n  X_L>X_C  \n Positive imaginary part \n Inductive; current lags voltage \n \n \n  X_L<X_C  \n Negative imaginary part \n Capacitive; current leads voltage \n \n \n  X_L=X_C  \n  Z=R  \n Resistive terminal behavior; voltage/current in phase \n \n \n \n The impedance triangle has horizontal side R, signed vertical side X, and hypotenuse  |Z| . It lets you connect resistance, reactance, magnitude, and angle. \n The slide's special cases are R alone, L alone, C alone, RL, RC, and LC. Their impedances follow by omitting absent elements: \n  R ,  jX_L ,  -jX_C ,  R+jX_L ,  R-jX_C , and  j(X_L-X_C) . \n Teacher's impedance exercises, worked here \n \n \n \n Given \n Derived R and X \n \n \n \n \n  Z=300+j500 Ω  \n  R=300 Ω ,  X=500 Ω  \n \n \n  Ũ=50∠30° V ,  Ĩ=2∠30° A  \n  Z=25 Ω , so  R=25 Ω ,  X=0  \n \n \n  Ũ=100∠-30° V ,  Ĩ=4∠40° A  \n  Z=25∠-70° Ω , so  R≈8.55 Ω ,  X≈-23.49 Ω  \n \n \n \n General series impedances \n Group an RL section as  Z₁=R₁+jX_L  and an RC section as  Z₂=R₂-jX_C . Then \n  Z_total=Z₁+Z₂ ,  Ĩ=Ũ/(Z₁+Z₂) . \n The phasor voltage-divider relationships are \n  Ũ₁=[Z₁/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ũ  \n  Ũ₂=[Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ũ . \n  Teacher example, worked here:   Ũ_s=10∠0° V ,  Z₁=3+j2 Ω ,  Z₂=2+j3 Ω . \n  Z_total=5+j5 Ω , so  Ĩ=10/(5+j5)=1-j1=√2∠-45° A . \n The section voltages are  Ũ₁=5-j1 V  and  Ũ₂=5+j1 V ; their sum is the supplied 10 V. Their magnitudes should not be added arithmetically. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "3.5", "chapter": 3, "title": "Parallel AC circuits", "html": "<p><em>Source: 10 pages, including two impedance examples.</em></p>\n<p>All parallel branches share the same voltage. Apply KCL to <strong>current phasors</strong>:</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ=Ĩ_R+Ĩ_L+Ĩ_C</code>.</p>\n<p>With voltage chosen at 0°, resistor current lies on the real axis, inductor current points at -90°, and capacitor current points at +90°.</p>\n<h3>Teacher's three 5 A examples</h3>\n<p><img alt=\"Original course slide, lesson 3.5, page 4: the three current-addition examples.\" src=\"assets/parallel-current-examples.png\" /></p>\n<p><em>Original course slide, lesson 3.5, page 4: the three current-addition examples.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parallel branches, each taking 5 A</th>\n<th>Total phasor</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Supply RMS current</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>R, L, and C</td>\n<td><code>5-j5+j5</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5 A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L and C only</td>\n<td><code>-j5+j5</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0 A in the ideal cancellation case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R and C only</td>\n<td><code>5+j5</code></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><code>5√2≈7.07 A</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the zero-total case, individual reactive branch currents still flow and exchange energy. It is the supply's net reactive current that cancels.</p>\n<h3>General parallel impedances</h3>\n<p>For two branches:</p>\n<p><code>Z_eq=Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)</code></p>\n<p><code>Ĩ=Ũ/Z_eq</code></p>\n<p><code>Ĩ₁=[Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ĩ</code>, <code>Ĩ₂=[Z₁/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ĩ</code>.</p>\n<p>Current division uses the <strong>opposite branch's impedance</strong> in the numerator. Use complex arithmetic; do not substitute only magnitudes.</p>\n<p><strong>Teacher E1, worked here:</strong> <code>Ĩ_s=5∠0° A</code>, <code>Z₁=2+j2 Ω</code>, <code>Z₂=3+j3 Ω</code>.</p>\n<p>Because both impedances have the same angle, their current-divider ratios are real: <code>Ĩ₁=3∠0° A</code>, <code>Ĩ₂=2∠0° A</code>. The equivalent is <code>1.2+j1.2 Ω</code>, so the common voltage is <code>Ũ=6+j6 V</code>.</p>\n<h3>Teacher E2: solve in two ways</h3>\n<p>Given <code>U=220 V</code>, the left branch is <code>Z₁=20-j114 Ω</code> and the right branch is <code>Z₂=40+j157 Ω</code>. Choose <code>Ũ=220∠0° V</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Method 1 - branch currents:</strong></p>\n<p><code>Ĩ₁=220/(20-j114)=0.32846+j1.87220 A</code></p>\n<p><code>Ĩ₂=220/(40+j157)=0.33525-j1.31586 A</code>.</p>\n<p>Add them:</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ=0.66371+j0.55634≈0.8660∠39.97° A</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Method 2 - total impedance:</strong></p>\n<p><code>Z_eq=Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)≈194.682-j163.188 Ω</code></p>\n<p><code>Z_eq≈254.03∠-39.97° Ω</code>.</p>\n<p>Dividing 220 V by this equivalent gives the same current. The load is net capacitive, so current leads the supply voltage.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide rounding:</strong> the teacher rounds branch polar forms early and reports approximately <code>0.862∠39.6° A</code> and <code>255∠-39.6° Ω</code>. The values above retain the stated component values until the end; the small numerical difference comes from intermediate rounding.</p>", "text": "  Source: 10 pages, including two impedance examples.  \n All parallel branches share the same voltage. Apply KCL to  current phasors : \n  Ĩ=Ĩ_R+Ĩ_L+Ĩ_C . \n With voltage chosen at 0°, resistor current lies on the real axis, inductor current points at -90°, and capacitor current points at +90°. \n Teacher's three 5 A examples \n   \n  Original course slide, lesson 3.5, page 4: the three current-addition examples.  \n \n \n \n Parallel branches, each taking 5 A \n Total phasor \n Supply RMS current \n \n \n \n \n R, L, and C \n  5-j5+j5  \n 5 A \n \n \n L and C only \n  -j5+j5  \n 0 A in the ideal cancellation case \n \n \n R and C only \n  5+j5  \n  5√2≈7.07 A  \n \n \n \n In the zero-total case, individual reactive branch currents still flow and exchange energy. It is the supply's net reactive current that cancels. \n General parallel impedances \n For two branches: \n  Z_eq=Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)  \n  Ĩ=Ũ/Z_eq  \n  Ĩ₁=[Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ĩ ,  Ĩ₂=[Z₁/(Z₁+Z₂)]Ĩ . \n Current division uses the  opposite branch's impedance  in the numerator. Use complex arithmetic; do not substitute only magnitudes. \n  Teacher E1, worked here:   Ĩ_s=5∠0° A ,  Z₁=2+j2 Ω ,  Z₂=3+j3 Ω . \n Because both impedances have the same angle, their current-divider ratios are real:  Ĩ₁=3∠0° A ,  Ĩ₂=2∠0° A . The equivalent is  1.2+j1.2 Ω , so the common voltage is  Ũ=6+j6 V . \n Teacher E2: solve in two ways \n Given  U=220 V , the left branch is  Z₁=20-j114 Ω  and the right branch is  Z₂=40+j157 Ω . Choose  Ũ=220∠0° V . \n  Method 1 - branch currents:  \n  Ĩ₁=220/(20-j114)=0.32846+j1.87220 A  \n  Ĩ₂=220/(40+j157)=0.33525-j1.31586 A . \n Add them: \n  Ĩ=0.66371+j0.55634≈0.8660∠39.97° A . \n  Method 2 - total impedance:  \n  Z_eq=Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)≈194.682-j163.188 Ω  \n  Z_eq≈254.03∠-39.97° Ω . \n Dividing 220 V by this equivalent gives the same current. The load is net capacitive, so current leads the supply voltage. \n  Slide rounding:  the teacher rounds branch polar forms early and reports approximately  0.862∠39.6° A  and  255∠-39.6° Ω . The values above retain the stated component values until the end; the small numerical difference comes from intermediate rounding. ", "minutes": 2}, {"id": "3.6", "chapter": 3, "title": "Power in AC circuits", "html": "<p><em>Source: 10 pages, including pure-element power, general power, units, addition, and the power triangle.</em></p>\n<p>Instantaneous power is <code>p(t)=u(t)i(t)</code>. Positive absorbed power transfers energy into an element; negative absorbed power returns energy to the circuit. Average power is the cycle average:</p>\n<p><code>P=(1/T)∫₀ᵀ p(t)dt</code>.</p>\n<h3>Pure resistor</h3>\n<p>For <code>u=√2 U sinωt</code> and <code>i=√2 I sinωt</code>:</p>\n<p><code>p=2UI sin²ωt=UI(1-cos2ωt)</code>.</p>\n<p>Power is never negative and oscillates at twice the electrical frequency. Its average is</p>\n<p><code>P=UI=I²R=U²/R</code>, with <code>Q=0</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide correction:</strong> the last line of the instantaneous-power derivation prints <code>1-cos²ωt</code>; the correct identity is <code>1-cos(2ωt)</code>.</p>\n<h3>Pure inductor</h3>\n<p>Choose <code>i=√2 I sinωt</code>. Voltage leads by 90°, so <code>u=√2 U cosωt</code> and</p>\n<p><code>p=UI sin2ωt</code>.</p>\n<p>It alternates between storage and return of magnetic energy. Its cycle average is <code>P=0</code>. With the usual signed reactive-power convention:</p>\n<p><code>Q_L=+UI=I²X_L=U²/X_L</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide correction:</strong> the pure-inductor page accidentally repeats the resistor's instantaneous and average-power formulas. The plotted alternating power, the text about energy exchange, and the final summary table correctly imply zero average real power for an ideal inductor.</p>\n<h3>Pure capacitor</h3>\n<p>Choose <code>u=√2 U sinωt</code>; then <code>i=√2 I cosωt</code>, giving <code>p=UI sin2ωt</code> for that chosen time reference. Energy alternates between storage in the electric field and return to the circuit; again <code>P=0</code>.</p>\n<p>The reactive-power <strong>magnitude</strong> is <code>UI=I²X_C=U²/X_C</code>. The standard signed value is</p>\n<p><code>Q_C=-UI</code>.</p>\n<p>The slide prints a positive capacitor Q as a magnitude. When adding inductive and capacitive reactive powers, use opposite signs. Instantaneous-power expressions must be compared using the same waveform reference; choosing a different reference changes the displayed sine's sign or phase, not the physical energy exchange.</p>\n<h3>General circuit power</h3>\n<p>Define <code>φ=ψ_u-ψ_i</code>. Using RMS U and I:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Unit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Active, real, or average power</td>\n<td><code>P=UI cosφ</code></td>\n<td>W, kW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signed reactive power</td>\n<td><code>Q=UI sinφ</code></td>\n<td>var, kvar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apparent power</td>\n<td><code>S=UI=√(P²+Q²)</code></td>\n<td>VA, kVA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power factor</td>\n<td><code>PF=P/S=cosφ</code></td>\n<td>Dimensionless</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For a passive load, positive Q is inductive and negative Q is capacitive. State <strong>lagging</strong> or <strong>leading</strong> as well as the numerical power factor, because the cosine alone cannot distinguish them.</p>\n<p>The teacher resolves current into an in-phase active component of magnitude <code>I_P=I cosφ</code> and a quadrature component associated with <code>I sinφ</code>. With voltage at 0°, the consistent rectangular expression is</p>\n<p><code>Ĩ=I cosφ-jI sinφ</code>.</p>\n<p>Thus <code>P=U I_P</code>, while the sign of Q distinguishes the quadrature direction. Some slide drawings show a leading-current arrow while using an unsigned angle; use the explicit <code>φ=ψ_u-ψ_i</code> definition to avoid sign ambiguity.</p>\n<h3>Power addition and triangle</h3>\n<p>For components or branches at the same frequency:</p>\n<p><code>P_total=ΣP_k</code>, <code>Q_total=ΣQ_k</code>.</p>\n<p>Then <code>S_total=√(P_total²+Q_total²)</code>. Apparent-power magnitudes do <strong>not</strong> generally add: <code>S_total≠ΣS_k</code>.</p>\n<p>The power triangle places P horizontally, signed Q vertically, and S on the hypotenuse. Consequently <code>cosφ=P/S</code> and <code>tanφ=Q/P</code> when the ratio is defined.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked here to illustrate the slide's addition rule:</strong> an ideal resistor absorbing 300 W and an ideal inductor taking 400 var have total apparent power <code>√(300²+400²)=500 VA</code>, not 700 VA. Adding an ideal capacitor with <code>Q=-400 var</code> cancels the net reactive power, leaving 300 VA for that ideal combined load.</p>\n<h3>What this document covers</h3>\n<p>The reviewed power document teaches instantaneous, average, reactive, and apparent power and their combination. It does not contain a separate capacitor-sizing or power-factor-correction procedure. Such a procedure in the earlier outline-based guide was extra material, not a summary of these slides.</p>", "text": "  Source: 10 pages, including pure-element power, general power, units, addition, and the power triangle.  \n Instantaneous power is  p(t)=u(t)i(t) . Positive absorbed power transfers energy into an element; negative absorbed power returns energy to the circuit. Average power is the cycle average: \n  P=(1/T)∫₀ᵀ p(t)dt . \n Pure resistor \n For  u=√2 U sinωt  and  i=√2 I sinωt : \n  p=2UI sin²ωt=UI(1-cos2ωt) . \n Power is never negative and oscillates at twice the electrical frequency. Its average is \n  P=UI=I²R=U²/R , with  Q=0 . \n  Slide correction:  the last line of the instantaneous-power derivation prints  1-cos²ωt ; the correct identity is  1-cos(2ωt) . \n Pure inductor \n Choose  i=√2 I sinωt . Voltage leads by 90°, so  u=√2 U cosωt  and \n  p=UI sin2ωt . \n It alternates between storage and return of magnetic energy. Its cycle average is  P=0 . With the usual signed reactive-power convention: \n  Q_L=+UI=I²X_L=U²/X_L . \n  Slide correction:  the pure-inductor page accidentally repeats the resistor's instantaneous and average-power formulas. The plotted alternating power, the text about energy exchange, and the final summary table correctly imply zero average real power for an ideal inductor. \n Pure capacitor \n Choose  u=√2 U sinωt ; then  i=√2 I cosωt , giving  p=UI sin2ωt  for that chosen time reference. Energy alternates between storage in the electric field and return to the circuit; again  P=0 . \n The reactive-power  magnitude  is  UI=I²X_C=U²/X_C . The standard signed value is \n  Q_C=-UI . \n The slide prints a positive capacitor Q as a magnitude. When adding inductive and capacitive reactive powers, use opposite signs. Instantaneous-power expressions must be compared using the same waveform reference; choosing a different reference changes the displayed sine's sign or phase, not the physical energy exchange. \n General circuit power \n Define  φ=ψ_u-ψ_i . Using RMS U and I: \n \n \n \n Quantity \n Formula \n Unit \n \n \n \n \n Active, real, or average power \n  P=UI cosφ  \n W, kW \n \n \n Signed reactive power \n  Q=UI sinφ  \n var, kvar \n \n \n Apparent power \n  S=UI=√(P²+Q²)  \n VA, kVA \n \n \n Power factor \n  PF=P/S=cosφ  \n Dimensionless \n \n \n \n For a passive load, positive Q is inductive and negative Q is capacitive. State  lagging  or  leading  as well as the numerical power factor, because the cosine alone cannot distinguish them. \n The teacher resolves current into an in-phase active component of magnitude  I_P=I cosφ  and a quadrature component associated with  I sinφ . With voltage at 0°, the consistent rectangular expression is \n  Ĩ=I cosφ-jI sinφ . \n Thus  P=U I_P , while the sign of Q distinguishes the quadrature direction. Some slide drawings show a leading-current arrow while using an unsigned angle; use the explicit  φ=ψ_u-ψ_i  definition to avoid sign ambiguity. \n Power addition and triangle \n For components or branches at the same frequency: \n  P_total=ΣP_k ,  Q_total=ΣQ_k . \n Then  S_total=√(P_total²+Q_total²) . Apparent-power magnitudes do  not  generally add:  S_total≠ΣS_k . \n The power triangle places P horizontally, signed Q vertically, and S on the hypotenuse. Consequently  cosφ=P/S  and  tanφ=Q/P  when the ratio is defined. \n  Worked here to illustrate the slide's addition rule:  an ideal resistor absorbing 300 W and an ideal inductor taking 400 var have total apparent power  √(300²+400²)=500 VA , not 700 VA. Adding an ideal capacitor with  Q=-400 var  cancels the net reactive power, leaving 300 VA for that ideal combined load. \n What this document covers \n The reviewed power document teaches instantaneous, average, reactive, and apparent power and their combination. It does not contain a separate capacitor-sizing or power-factor-correction procedure. Such a procedure in the earlier outline-based guide was extra material, not a summary of these slides. ", "minutes": 3}];
